window.TOPICS=window.TOPICS||{};window.TOPICS['pca']={"id": "pca", "title": "Principal Component Analysis", "tagline": "Covariance, PCs, projections, reconstruction, dual PCA. 42 PYQs (121 marks) solved step by step.", "concepts": {"var": {"name": "Variance along PCs and axes", "marks": 31, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Variance along a direction is how spread out the shadows of the points are when you project them onto that line. For a unit vector $u$ it is $u^TCu$. Along an eigenvector it is exactly the eigenvalue; along any other direction it lies between the smallest and largest eigenvalue.</p>"}, "cov": {"name": "Building the covariance matrix", "marks": 7, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Three moves: subtract the mean from every point, form $\\tilde x\\tilde x^T$ for each point (a small $d\\times d$ table), add the tables and divide by $n$. Diagonal = variances of single features, off-diagonal = how two features move together.</p>"}, "dirs": {"name": "PC directions and orthogonality", "marks": 11, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Principal directions are the eigenvectors of $C$, sorted by eigenvalue. Because $C$ is symmetric they are orthogonal. Each one is only fixed up to sign: $u$ and $-u$ describe the same line.</p>"}, "coord": {"name": "Projection scores (coordinates)", "marks": 24, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>A score is just a dot product: subtract the mean, then dot with the unit direction. If the direction you are given is not unit length, divide by its length first.</p>"}, "evr": {"name": "Explained variance", "marks": 10, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Each eigenvalue is a slice of the total variance pie. Explained variance of a component is its slice over the whole pie; add slices for the cumulative ratio, then stop at the first $k$ that reaches the target.</p>"}, "recon": {"name": "Reconstruction and residues", "marks": 13, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Reconstruction walks back from the scores: start at the mean and add each kept score times its direction. What you lose is everything along the dropped directions, and on average that is the sum of the dropped eigenvalues.</p>"}, "storage": {"name": "Storage after compression", "marks": 5, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Count numbers, not points: originally $n\\times d$. After compression you keep $k$ scores per point ($k\\times n$) plus the $k$ direction vectors ($k\\times d$), and the mean ($d$) if the question includes it.</p>"}, "rank": {"name": "Rank and zero eigenvalues", "marks": 7, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Centred points add up to zero, so $n$ points span at most $n-1$ directions. The covariance matrix can never have more than $\\min(d,n-1)$ non-zero eigenvalues; the rest are zero.</p>"}, "dual": {"name": "Dual PCA ($XX^T$ vs $X^TX$)", "marks": 13, "ease": 1, "repair": "<p>When there are fewer points than features, work with the small $n\\times n$ table of dot products $K=\\tilde X^T\\tilde X$ instead of the $d\\times d$ covariance. The two share their non-zero eigenvalues (up to the factor $n$), and the directions can be rebuilt from $K$'s eigenvectors.</p>"}}, "sections": [{"id": "orient", "title": "Orientation: what PCA is for", "html": "<p>PCA answers: <b>if I may keep only a few numbers per data point, which few numbers lose the least information?</b> It finds new axes (directions) along which the data varies most, and describes every point by its position along the first few of those axes.</p><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Where this sits</div><p>PCA is the first unsupervised method here. It needs only linear algebra: the covariance matrix and its eigenvectors. After it come kernels (kernel PCA replaces dot products by kernel values) and clustering.</p></div><h3>What you will be able to do</h3><ol><li>Centre data and compute a covariance matrix.</li><li>Find principal directions as eigenvectors, and variances as eigenvalues.</li><li>Project points, compute coordinates and reconstructions.</li><li>Compute explained variance, choose $k$, count storage and rank.</li></ol><h3>Prerequisite check</h3><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Mean of $1,3,5$ and of $2,4,6$</summary><p>$3$ and $4$. The mean vector is $(3,4)$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Length of $u=(1,1)/\\sqrt2$</summary><p>$\\sqrt{\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12}=1$: a unit vector. Projection formulas assume $\\|u\\|=1$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Eigenvector in one line</summary><p>$Cu=\\lambda u$: multiplying $u$ by the matrix only stretches it, by the factor $\\lambda$.</p></details><h3>PYQ sources</h3><p>Converted from the Quiz 1 PCA notes. The teaching (intuition, widgets, worked examples) stays on these Quiz 1 pages; every PYQ below is solved here in the Quiz 2 format with each option explained.</p><ul><li><a href=\"#q1-b04\">PCA prerequisites</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b05\">2.1 Covariance and eigenvalues (A)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b06\">2.1 (B)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b07\">2.2 PC directions and orthogonality</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b08\">2.3 Projection scores (A)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b09\">2.3 (B)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b10\">2.4 Explained variance and rank</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b11\">2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b12\">2.6 Dual PCA</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-rev2\">T2 revision and cheat sheet</a></li></ul><p>Convention in this course: covariance $C=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\tilde x_i\\tilde x_i^T$ with centred data. A few Quiz 1 PYQs whose data or options are missing from the source copy stay on the Quiz 1 pages only. Questions tagged [FLAG] or [?] have a key that disagrees with the printed data or no key in the source.</p>"}, {"id": "var", "title": "2.0 Variance, centring and the covariance matrix", "html": "<h3>Intuition</h3><p>A direction is useful if the data is spread out along it: points that differ a lot along it can be told apart. A direction along which every point has almost the same value tells you nothing. So PCA looks for directions of <b>maximum variance</b>.</p><div class=\"box analogy\"><div class=\"bt\">Everyday analogy</div><p>Photographing a long, thin pencil: from the side you capture its length (large spread), from the end you capture a dot (almost no spread). PCA picks the side view. <b>Where the analogy stops:</b> PCA picks the best direction by calculation, not by eye, and works in any number of dimensions.</p></div><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Definitions</div><p>Data points $x_1,\\dots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$. <b>Mean</b> $\\mu=\\tfrac1n\\sum_ix_i$. <b>Centred data</b> $\\tilde x_i=x_i-\\mu$ (move the cloud so its centre is at the origin). <b>Covariance matrix</b> $$C=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\tilde x_i\\tilde x_i^T\\quad(d\\times d).$$ Entry $C_{jj}$ is the variance of feature $j$; entry $C_{jl}$ is the covariance of features $j$ and $l$. $C$ is symmetric and positive semidefinite. The variance of the data along a unit vector $u$ is $u^TCu$. (The course uses the factor $\\tfrac1n$; $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ appears only if the question says so.)</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 1 (simple): four points</div><p>Centred points $(2,0),(0,2),(-2,0),(0,-2)$. $C=\\tfrac14\\begin{bmatrix}8&0\\\\0&8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Equal variance in both directions: no direction is preferred.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 2 (exam level): centre first</div><p>Points $(1,2),(3,4),(5,6)$. $\\mu=(3,4)$. Centred: $(-2,-2),(0,0),(2,2)$. $C=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}8&8\\\\8&8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8/3&8/3\\\\8/3&8/3\\end{bmatrix}$.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Not centring first (the answer then depends on where the data sits).</li><li>Dividing by $n-1$ when the course convention is $n$.</li><li>Calling $x^Tx$ the covariance (for a $d\\times d$ matrix use $\\sum\\tilde x\\tilde x^T$).</li></ul></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Rotate the direction and watch the variance of the projected points change. Its maximum is at the top eigenvector.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"vardir\" data-points=\"2,1;-2,-1;1,2;-1,-2;1,-1;-1,1\" data-theta=\"0\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq1", "concept": "var", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$C=\\operatorname{diag}(5,2)$, $u=(0.6,0.8)$. Variance along $u$?", "num": {"ans": 3.08, "tol": 0.002, "show": "3.08"}, "explain": "$5(0.36)+2(0.64)=3.08$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq2", "concept": "var", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Variance along the $x$-axis $(1,0)$?", "num": {"ans": 2, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2"}, "explain": "$u^TCu=C_{11}=2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq3", "concept": "var", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "The variance of the data along PC1 equals", "options": [{"t": "the largest eigenvalue of $C$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "the trace of $C$", "ok": false, "why": "That is the total variance."}, {"t": "the largest entry of $C$", "ok": false, "why": "Not in general."}, {"t": "$1$", "ok": false, "why": "Only by accident."}]}, {"id": "pq4", "concept": "cov", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$. Entry $C_{12}$ (divide by $n$)?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "Centred $(-1,-1),(1,1)$: $(1+1)/2=1$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq5", "concept": "cov", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "A covariance matrix is always", "options": [{"t": "symmetric and positive semidefinite", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "diagonal", "ok": false, "why": "Off-diagonal covariances can be non-zero."}, {"t": "invertible", "ok": false, "why": "It is singular when points lie on a lower-dimensional set."}, {"t": "with all entries positive", "ok": false, "why": "Covariances can be negative."}]}]}, {"id": "cov", "title": "2.1 Eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the covariance matrix", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">The key fact</div><p>The unit vector $u$ that maximises the variance $u^TCu$ is the eigenvector of $C$ with the largest eigenvalue, and the maximum variance equals that eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$. The next best direction (orthogonal to the first) is the eigenvector of $\\lambda_2$, and so on.</p></div><p><b>Why.</b> If $Cu=\\lambda u$ with $\\|u\\|=1$ then $u^TCu=\\lambda u^Tu=\\lambda$. Among all unit vectors, the Rayleigh quotient $u^TCu$ is largest for the top eigenvector.</p><h3>Facts to know</h3><table><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Eigenvalues of $C$ are $\\ge0$</td><td>$C$ is positive semidefinite (variances cannot be negative)</td></tr><tr><td>Eigenvectors are orthogonal (distinct eigenvalues)</td><td>$C$ is symmetric</td></tr><tr><td>$\\sum_j\\lambda_j=\\operatorname{tr}C$</td><td>total variance</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lambda_3\\le u^TCu\\le\\lambda_1$ for any unit $u$ (3 features)</td><td>variance along any direction lies between the smallest and largest eigenvalue</td></tr><tr><td>PC1 is unique only up to sign</td><td>$u$ and $-u$ give the same line</td></tr></table><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 3: eigen-decomposition</div><p>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}8/3&8/3\\\\8/3&8/3\\end{bmatrix}$ from example 2. Eigenvalues $16/3$ (vector $(1,1)/\\sqrt2$) and $0$ (vector $(1,-1)/\\sqrt2$). Check: $\\operatorname{tr}C=16/3=\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2$ ✓, and $u^TCu=16/3$ for $u=(1,1)/\\sqrt2$ ✓. The points all lie on a line, so the second direction carries no variance.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 4 (tricky): variance along a given direction</div><p>With $C=\\operatorname{diag}(5,2)$, the variance along the unit vector $u=(0.6,0.8)$ is $u^TCu=5(0.36)+2(0.64)=3.08$, between $2$ and $5$ as it must be. It is <b>not</b> the eigenvalue unless $u$ is an eigenvector.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Using a non-unit vector in $u^TCu$ (normalise first).</li><li>Thinking PC directions need not be orthogonal.</li><li>Thinking eigenvalues can be negative for a covariance matrix.</li><li>Thinking PC1 is unique including sign.</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq6", "concept": "dirs", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3&1\\\\1&3\\end{bmatrix}$. Largest eigenvalue?", "num": {"ans": 4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4"}, "explain": "Eigenvalues $3\\pm1$: $4$ (vector $(1,1)/\\sqrt2$) and $2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq7", "concept": "dirs", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "PC1 $=(1,1)/\\sqrt2$ in 2-D. PC2 must be", "options": [{"t": "$\\pm(1,-1)/\\sqrt2$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: orthogonal and unit length."}, {"t": "$(1,0)$", "ok": false, "why": "Not orthogonal to PC1."}, {"t": "$(1,1)/\\sqrt2$ again", "ok": false, "why": "Must be orthogonal."}, {"t": "any unit vector", "ok": false, "why": "It must be orthogonal to PC1."}]}, {"id": "pq8", "concept": "var", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Eigenvalues of $C$ are $4$ and $2$. Total variance?", "num": {"ans": 6, "tol": 0.001, "show": "6"}, "explain": "Trace $=4+2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq9", "concept": "cov", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(1,0),(3,0),(2,3)$. $C_{22}$ (divide by $n$)?", "num": {"ans": 2, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2"}, "explain": "Mean of second coordinate $1$; centred $-1,-1,2$; $(1+1+4)/3=2$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "coord", "title": "2.3 Projections, coordinates and reconstruction", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formulas</div><p>For a unit principal direction $u$ and a point $x$: <b>coordinate (score)</b> $z=u^T(x-\\mu)$. <b>Reconstruction from the first $k$ components</b> $\\hat x=\\mu+\\sum_{j=1}^kz_ju_j$. <b>Reconstruction error</b> (average squared) $=\\sum_{j>k}\\lambda_j$, the sum of the dropped eigenvalues.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 5: coordinate and reconstruction</div><p>Data of example 2, $u=(1,1)/\\sqrt2$, $\\mu=(3,4)$, point $x=(4,6)$. $x-\\mu=(1,2)$. $z=(1+2)/\\sqrt2=2.121$. Reconstruction with one component: $\\hat x=\\mu+zu=(3,4)+2.121\\,(0.707,0.707)=(4.5,5.5)$. The error vector is $(-0.5,0.5)$, along the dropped direction.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 6 (tricky): error = dropped eigenvalues</div><p>Eigenvalues $5,3,1,1$. Keeping $2$ components loses $1+1=2$ units of variance, out of $10$ in total, so $20\\%$ of the variance is lost.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Forgetting to subtract $\\mu$ before projecting, or to add it back when reconstructing.</li><li>Using a non-unit $u$.</li><li>Mixing the score $z$ (a number) with the reconstructed point (a vector).</li></ul></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Change the point and the direction: the explorer shows the projection, the score and the residual vector.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"proj2d\" data-x=\"3,4\" data-w=\"1,1\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq10", "concept": "coord", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\mu=(0,0)$, $u=(0.6,0.8)$, $x=(5,0)$. Score $z$?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$5(0.6)+0=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq11", "concept": "coord", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\mu=(1,1)$, $u=(1,0)$, $x=(4,7)$. Score $z$?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$(4-1)\\cdot1+(7-1)\\cdot0=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq12", "concept": "recon", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Same as the first: $\\mu=0$, $u=(0.6,0.8)$, $x=(5,0)$. First coordinate of the one-component reconstruction?", "num": {"ans": 1.8, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1.8"}, "explain": "$\\hat x=zu=3(0.6,0.8)=(1.8,2.4)$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq13", "concept": "recon", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Same point: squared distance between $x$ and its reconstruction?", "num": {"ans": 16, "tol": 0.001, "show": "16"}, "explain": "$(5-1.8)^2+(0-2.4)^2=10.24+5.76=16$ ($=\\|x\\|^2-z^2=25-9$).", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "evr", "title": "2.4 Explained variance, choosing k, storage and rank", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formulas</div><p><b>Explained variance ratio</b> of component $j$: $\\lambda_j/\\sum_l\\lambda_l$. <b>Cumulative</b> for the first $k$: $\\sum_{j\\le k}\\lambda_j/\\sum_l\\lambda_l$. Choose the smallest $k$ whose cumulative ratio reaches the target (for example $90\\%$).</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 7: cumulative ratio</div><p>Eigenvalues $2,1,0.5$ (total $3.5$). Ratios $0.571,0.286,0.143$. First two components: $3/3.5=0.857$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 8: storage</div><p>$n=100$ points in $d=10$ dimensions: $1000$ numbers. Keep $k=2$ components: scores $2\\times100=200$ plus the two directions $2\\times10=20$ (plus the mean, $10$ numbers): $220$ (without the mean). So compression is worthwhile when $k(n+d)<nd$.</p></div><h3>Rank</h3><p>The rank of $C$ is at most $\\min(d,\\,n-1)$: centred points satisfy $\\sum\\tilde x_i=0$, which removes one dimension. With $n\\le d$ points there are at most $n-1$ non-zero eigenvalues.</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 9 (tricky): few points</div><p>$n=3$ points in $d=10$: $C$ has at most $2$ non-zero eigenvalues, so two components explain $100\\%$ of the variance.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Ratio computed from standard deviations instead of variances (eigenvalues).</li><li>Forgetting the $n-1$ cap on the rank.</li><li>Treating the cumulative ratio as per-component.</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq14", "concept": "evr", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Eigenvalues $3,1,0.5,0.5$. Smallest $k$ with cumulative ratio at least $80\\%$?", "num": {"ans": 2, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2"}, "explain": "$3/5=60\\%$, $4/5=80\\%$: $k=2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq15", "concept": "storage", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=50$, $d=20$, $k=3$. Numbers stored (scores plus directions, no mean)?", "num": {"ans": 210, "tol": 0.001, "show": "210"}, "explain": "$3\\cdot50+3\\cdot20=210$ versus $1000$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq16", "concept": "storage", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Ignoring the mean, compression saves space when", "options": [{"t": "$k(n+d)<nd$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "$k<d$ always", "ok": false, "why": "Not sufficient: $k=d-1$ with small $n$ can cost more."}, {"t": "$k<n$", "ok": false, "why": "Not the right count."}, {"t": "$n<d$", "ok": false, "why": "Unrelated."}]}, {"id": "pq17", "concept": "rank", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=5$ points in $d=3$. Maximum non-zero eigenvalues of $C$?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$\\min(3,4)=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq18", "concept": "rank", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=2$ points in $d=100$. Maximum non-zero eigenvalues of $C$?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "$\\min(100,1)=1$: two centred points are $\\pm v$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "dual", "title": "2.5 Dual PCA (preview of kernel PCA)", "html": "<p>Instead of the $d\\times d$ covariance matrix, use the $n\\times n$ Gram matrix $K=\\tilde X^T\\tilde X$ with $K_{ij}=\\tilde x_i^T\\tilde x_j$ (columns of $\\tilde X$ are the centred points). If $K\\beta=\\lambda\\beta$ with $\\|\\beta\\|=1$, then $\\alpha=\\beta/\\sqrt\\lambda$, the PC variance is $\\lambda/n$, and the training scores are $\\sqrt\\lambda\\,\\beta_i$. This is useful when $d\\gg n$, and it needs only dot products, which is what lets kernels replace them.</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 10</div><p>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$ has eigenvalues $9,1$: PC variances $9/2=4.5$ and $0.5$.</p></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq19", "concept": "dual", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Dual PCA with $n=4$: the top eigenvalue of $K=\\tilde X^T\\tilde X$ is $12$. Variance of PC1?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$12/4=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "pq20", "concept": "dual", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Dual PCA is cheaper when", "options": [{"t": "$d\\gg n$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $K$ is $n\\times n$."}, {"t": "$n\\gg d$", "ok": false, "why": "Then $C$ ($d\\times d$) is smaller."}, {"t": "$n=d$", "ok": false, "why": "Same size."}, {"t": "never", "ok": false, "why": "It is when $d$ is large."}]}, {"id": "pq21", "concept": "dual", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "$XX^T$ ($3\\times3$) has eigenvalues $9,1,0$. The non-zero eigenvalues of $X^TX$ ($2\\times2$) are", "options": [{"t": "$9$ and $1$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: the two products share non-zero eigenvalues."}, {"t": "$3$ and $1$", "ok": false, "why": "Those are square roots."}, {"t": "$9,1,0$", "ok": false, "why": "$X^TX$ is $2\\times2$."}, {"t": "unknown", "ok": false, "why": "They are known."}]}]}, {"id": "pyqs", "title": "All PYQs of this topic, solved", "kind": "pyq", "html": ""}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Revision page", "html": "<h3>Formulas</h3><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>Centring</td><td>$\\tilde x=x-\\mu$</td></tr><tr><td>Covariance</td><td>$C=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\tilde x\\tilde x^T$</td></tr><tr><td>Variance along unit $u$</td><td>$u^TCu$</td></tr><tr><td>Score</td><td>$z=u^T(x-\\mu)$</td></tr><tr><td>Reconstruction</td><td>$\\mu+\\sum_{j\\le k}z_ju_j$</td></tr><tr><td>Error</td><td>$\\sum_{j>k}\\lambda_j$</td></tr><tr><td>EVR</td><td>$\\lambda_j/\\sum\\lambda$</td></tr><tr><td>Rank bound</td><td>$\\min(d,n-1)$</td></tr><tr><td>Dual PCA</td><td>$K\\beta=\\lambda\\beta$, variance $\\lambda/n$</td></tr></table><h3>Exam decision flow</h3><ol><li>Centre first. Always.</li><li>Covariance: $\\tfrac1n\\sum\\tilde x\\tilde x^T$.</li><li>Eigenvalues: sort, use trace as a check.</li><li>Unit-length directions before projecting.</li><li>Variance lost = dropped eigenvalues.</li></ol>"}, {"id": "test", "title": "Topic test: mixed questions", "kind": "test", "html": ""}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "Checklist: skills you should be able to do", "kind": "checklist", "html": ""}], "pyqs": [{"id": "pc01", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "var", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (1 and 3) is derived by proof [?].", "wording": "Select all true statements regarding standard PCA applied on a centered dataset.", "options": ["The variance of the dataset along the first principal component is maximum.", "The variance of the dataset along the first principal component is minimum.", "The first principal component is the unit norm eigenvector corresponding to the largest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix.", "The first principal component is the unit norm eigenvector corresponding to the smallest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix."], "key": {"opts": [0, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements correctly describe the first principal component (PC1).</p>", "idea": "<p>PC1 is the unit direction along which the centred data has the largest variance; that direction is the eigenvector of $C$ with the largest eigenvalue.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>PC1 $=\\arg\\max_{\\|u\\|=1}u^TCu$. So \"variance along PC1 is maximum\" is true and \"minimum\" is false (options 1 and 2).</li><li>Write $u=\\sum_k c_kq_k$ with $\\sum c_k^2=1$. Then $u^TCu=\\sum_k\\lambda_kc_k^2$, a weighted average of eigenvalues, so it is at most $\\lambda_1$, with equality only at $u=q_1$. Hence PC1 is the unit eigenvector of the largest eigenvalue (option 3 true).</li><li>The smallest eigenvalue gives the direction of minimum variance (the last PC), not PC1: option 4 false.</li><li>Concrete check: $C=\\operatorname{diag}(3,1)$ gives variance $3$ along $e_1$, $1$ along $e_2$, $2$ along $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$. The maximum is at $e_1$, the largest eigenvalue direction.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Options 1/2 and 3/4 are contradictory pairs, so exactly one of each pair is true.</p>", "verify": "<p>Both the proof and the concrete $C=\\operatorname{diag}(3,1)$ give options 1 and 3.</p>", "trap": "Mixing up \"variance is maximum\" with \"reconstruction error is minimum\" (both hold at PC1). Remember the PC is always the unit-norm eigenvector.", "rule": "<p>PC1 $\\leftrightarrow$ maximum variance $\\leftrightarrow\\lambda_{\\max}$; last PC $\\leftrightarrow$ minimum variance $\\leftrightarrow\\lambda_{\\min}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred data, eigenvalues $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\lambda_3$. True? (a) every unit $u$ has $\\lambda_3\\le u^TCu\\le\\lambda_1$; (b) the PC3 direction has minimum variance; (c) PC1 is always unique up to sign.", "a": "(a) and (b) true. (c) false: if $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2$ any unit vector in their plane can be PC1 (for example $C=2I$)."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b> This is the definition of PC1.", "False: that describes the last PC.", "<b>True.</b> The maximiser is the top eigenvector.", "False: the smallest eigenvalue belongs to the minimum-variance direction."]}}, {"id": "pc02", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "wording": "Consider a dataset that has 4 points in $\\mathbb R^2$ that lie on a line passing through the origin. $$D=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\\\-30\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\\\-15\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5\\\\15\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}10\\\\30\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$$ If normal PCA is performed on this dataset, what is the variance along the first principal component?", "key": {"lo": 625, "hi": 625, "show": "625"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1$, the variance along PC1.</p>", "idea": "<p>The mean is $(0,0)$ already. All points lie on one line, so the covariance matrix has rank 1: $\\lambda_2=0$ and $\\lambda_1=\\operatorname{tr}C=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|\\tilde x_i\\|^2$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Mean: $\\tfrac14(-10-5+5+10,\\,-30-15+15+30)=(0,0)$, so the data is already centred.</li><li>Squared norms: $100+900=1000$, $25+225=250$, $250$, $1000$. Sum $=2500$.</li><li>Rank 1, so $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac{2500}4=625$.</li><li>Cross-check with the full matrix: $\\sum x^2=250$, $\\sum xy=750$, $\\sum y^2=2250$, so $C=\\begin{bmatrix}62.5&187.5\\\\187.5&562.5\\end{bmatrix}$. $\\operatorname{tr}=625$, $\\det=62.5\\cdot562.5-187.5^2=0$, so eigenvalues $625$ and $0$.</li><li>Second cross-check: PC1 $=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt{10}}(1,3)$; scores are $\\mp10\\sqrt{10},\\mp5\\sqrt{10}$, squares $1000,250,250,1000$, mean $625$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Using $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ would give $833.33$, which does not match the key; so the course uses $\\tfrac1n$. Convention in this course: covariance $C=\\tfrac1n\\sum_i\\tilde x_i\\tilde x_i^T$ with $\\tilde x_i=x_i-\\mu$ (confirmed by the key $625$ in the Oct 2023 line question).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: eigenvalues $[625,0]$. Bound check: $\\lambda_1\\ge C_{22}=562.5\\ge C_{11}=62.5$.</p>", "trap": "$833.33$ ($\\tfrac1{n-1}$); $562.5$ (variance of the $y$ feature, not PC1); $2500$ (forgot to divide by $n$); $25$ (took the standard deviation).", "rule": "<p>Points on a line: check the mean, then $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|\\tilde x_i\\|^2$. No eigen-solving needed.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(-6,8),(-3,4),(3,-4),(6,-8)$. Variance along PC1 and PC2?", "a": "Mean $(0,0)$; norms$^2$ $100,25,25,100$; $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac{250}4=62.5$, $\\lambda_2=0$."}}}, {"id": "pc03", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "wording": "Consider a covariance matrix $C$ for a mean-centered dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$. After standard PCA, the three principal components are $\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$. Assume $C$ is not the zero matrix. Which statements are true?", "options": ["$C$ is a diagonal matrix.", "The diagonal entries of $C$ are non-negative.", "The diagonal entries of $C$ are strictly greater than zero.", "$C$ has to be the identity matrix.", "$C$ is of the form $kI$, where $k>0$."], "key": {"opts": [0, 1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> what must be true of $C$ when its principal components are the standard basis vectors.</p>", "idea": "<p>The PCs are the eigenvectors of $C$. If they are $e_1,e_2,e_3$ then $Q=I$ and $C=Q\\Lambda Q^T=\\Lambda$ is diagonal with entries $\\lambda_k\\ge0$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$Ce_k=\\lambda_ke_k$ means the $k$-th column of $C$ is $\\lambda_ke_k$, so every off-diagonal entry is $0$: $C$ is diagonal (option 1 true).</li><li>The diagonal entries are $\\lambda_k=e_k^TCe_k=\\tfrac1n\\sum_i x_{ik}^2\\ge0$ (option 2 true).</li><li>\"Strictly greater than zero\" fails if a feature is constant (centred value $0$); $C\\ne0$ only says some entry is non-zero (option 3 false).</li><li>The eigenvalues are free (only $\\ge0$), so $C$ need not be $I$ or $kI$. Counterexamples: $\\operatorname{diag}(5,0,0)$ and $\\operatorname{diag}(3,2,1)$ (options 4 and 5 false).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>\"Possible\" is not \"necessary\": $C=kI$ is allowed, but it is not forced.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: eigenvalues of $\\operatorname{diag}(5,0,0)$ and $\\operatorname{diag}(3,2,1)$ are $[5,0,0]$ and $[3,2,1]$, both valid covariance matrices. The derived set $\\{1,2\\}$ matches the source key.</p>", "trap": "Reading \"$C\\ne0$\" as \"all entries non-zero\", or \"PCs are axes\" as \"data is round\".", "rule": "<p>PCs equal the axes $\\iff$ features uncorrelated $\\iff$ $C$ diagonal (entries $\\ge0$, zeros allowed).</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred 2-D data has PCs $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$ and $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$ with eigenvalues $5$ and $1$. Find $C$.", "a": "$C=Q\\Lambda Q^T=\\begin{bmatrix}3&2\\\\2&3\\end{bmatrix}$ (check: $a\\pm b=5,1$). Not diagonal, since the PCs are not the axes."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b> $C=\\Lambda$.", "<b>True.</b> They are variances.", "False: a zero entry is allowed.", "False: $\\operatorname{diag}(3,2,1)$ has these PCs and is not $I$.", "False: same counterexample."]}}, {"id": "pc04", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q10", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ is the covariance matrix of a mean-centered dataset. Standard PCA is performed. The first two PCs are $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$. (Original Q9, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Find the variance of the dataset along the first principal component.", "key": {"lo": 3, "hi": 3, "show": "3"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1$ for $C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The variance along PC$_k$ is $w_k^TCw_k=\\lambda_k$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Characteristic equation: $\\operatorname{tr}C=4$, $\\det C=4-1=3$, so $\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+3=0$, giving $\\lambda=3,1$.</li><li>Shortcut for $\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\b&a\\end{bmatrix}$: eigenvalues $a\\pm b$ on $(1,\\pm1)$, so $2\\pm1=3,1$.</li><li>Direct: $Cw_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(3,3)=3w_1$, so $w_1^TCw_1=3$.</li><li>Largest is $3$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Check: trace $3+1=4$, determinant $3\\cdot1=3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: eigenvalues $[3,1]$; key $3$.</p>", "trap": "$4$ is the total variance (trace); $2$ is the diagonal entry (the next question); $\\sqrt3$ is a standard deviation.", "rule": "<p>\"Along PC$_k$\" means eigenvalue; \"along an axis\" means a diagonal entry; \"total\" means the trace.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}5&2\\\\2&5\\end{bmatrix}$. Variance along PC1?", "a": "$a+b=7$ (PC1 $=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$), $\\lambda_2=3$; trace $10$."}}}, {"id": "pc05", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 1, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ is the covariance matrix of a mean-centered dataset. Standard PCA is performed. The first two PCs are $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$. (Original Q9, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Find the variance of the dataset along the x-axis.", "key": {"lo": 2, "hi": 2, "show": "2"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the variance along $e_1=(1,0)$, an axis rather than a PC.</p>", "idea": "<p>Variance along a unit direction $u$ is $u^TCu$; for $u=e_1$ this is the diagonal entry $C_{11}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$e_1^TCe_1=C_{11}=2$.</li><li>Second route: $e_1$ in the PC basis has coordinates $e_1^Tw_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$ and $e_1^Tw_2=-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$, weights $\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12$. Variance $=\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac12\\cdot1=2$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Bound check: $\\lambda_2=1\\le2\\le\\lambda_1=3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Both routes give $2$; key $2$.</p>", "trap": "$3$ (copying the PC1 answer), $1$ (the off-diagonal covariance), $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$ (a projection coefficient).", "rule": "<p>\"Along the x-axis (feature $j$)\": read $C_{jj}$; no eigen-work.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Same $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5&2\\\\2&5\\end{bmatrix}$: variance along the y-axis, and along $v=(3,4)$?", "a": "$C_{22}=5$. For $v$ use the unit vector $\\tfrac15(3,4)$: $\\tfrac1{25}(9\\cdot5+2\\cdot2\\cdot12+16\\cdot5)=\\tfrac{173}{25}=6.92$, inside $[3,7]$."}}}, {"id": "pc06", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "wording": "The eigenvalues of the covariance matrix of a centered dataset in $\\mathbb R^5$ are $30,\\ 10,\\ 10,\\ 0,\\ 0$. Standard PCA is performed. What percentage of the total variance is captured by the top two principal components?", "key": {"lo": 80, "hi": 80, "show": "80"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\tfrac{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}{\\sum\\lambda}\\times100$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Each PC carries variance equal to its eigenvalue, and total variance is the sum of all eigenvalues (the trace).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Top two: $30+10=40$.</li><li>Total: $30+10+10+0+0=50$.</li><li>$\\tfrac{40}{50}=0.8$, so $80\\%$.</li><li>Complement check: dropped $=10+0+0=10$, which is $20\\%$; $100-20=80$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The tie $\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=10$ does not matter: either choice gives the same top-two sum.</p>", "verify": "<p>Both routes give $80$; key $80$.</p>", "trap": "$60$ (only $\\lambda_1$), $40$ (the sum, not a percentage), $0.8$ (the question asks for a percentage), $100$ (three eigenvalues are non-zero, only two were taken).", "rule": "<p>\"% of total variance in the top $k$\": sort, add the top $k$, divide by the sum of all.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Eigenvalues $12,6,4,2,1,0$. Percentage captured by the top three PCs?", "a": "$\\tfrac{12+6+4}{25}=88\\%$."}}}, {"id": "pc07", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "tagNote": "Printed key $43$ to $44$ equals the uncentred PCA value $43.75$; the correct centred value is $18.44$. Both are shown. Concept-wise $18.44$ is right; this paper\\'s key did not centre.", "context": "Dataset: $S=\\{(0,0),(1,2),(3,6),(5,10)\\}$, four collinear points (Original Q12, comprehension, 0 marks).", "wording": "If standard PCA is performed on this dataset, what is the variance along the first principal component? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 43, "hi": 44, "show": "43 to 44"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1$ to two decimals for four collinear points whose mean is not zero.</p>", "idea": "<p>Standard PCA centres first. For collinear points $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|\\tilde x_i\\|^2$ with the <i>centred</i> points. The accepted range $43$ to $44$ matches the <i>uncentred</i> value instead.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Mean $=\\tfrac14(0+1+3+5,\\,0+2+6+10)=(2.25,4.5)$, not zero.</li><li>Centred points: $(-2.25,-4.5),(-1.25,-2.5),(0.75,1.5),(2.75,5.5)$. Since $y=2x$, $\\|\\tilde x\\|^2=5\\tilde x_1^2$: $25.3125,7.8125,2.8125,37.8125$; sum $73.75$.</li><li>Centred $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac{73.75}4=18.4375\\approx18.44$.</li><li>Uncentred (no centring): norms$^2$ $0,5,45,125$, sum $175$, so $\\tfrac{175}4=43.75$, inside the accepted range. The gap equals $\\|\\mu\\|^2=2.25^2+4.5^2=25.3125$.</li><li>Other conventions: centred with $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ gives $24.5833$; uncentred with $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ gives $58.3333$. Only the uncentred $\\tfrac1n$ value lies in the key range.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>1-D check: points are $k(1,2)$ with $k=0,1,3,5$; $\\operatorname{Var}(k)=\\tfrac{35}4-\\tfrac{81}{16}=\\tfrac{59}{16}$, and $5\\cdot\\tfrac{59}{16}=18.4375$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: centred $[18.4375,0]$; uncentred $[43.75,0]$. The key corresponds to not subtracting the mean.</p>", "trap": "Copying the previous question\\'s shortcut where the mean was $0$; here \"on a line\" does not mean \"centred\". Also $24.58$ ($\\tfrac1{n-1}$) and $4.29$ (standard deviation).", "rule": "<p>\"Line through the origin\" is not \"centred\": always compute the mean. If a key style is ambiguous, compute both values.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(1,1),(2,2),(4,4),(5,5)$. Centred $\\lambda_1$? And if someone forgets to centre?", "a": "Mean $(3,3)$; centred norms$^2$ $8,2,2,8$; $\\lambda_1=5$. Uncentred: $\\tfrac{2+8+32+50}4=23$. The gap is $\\|\\mu\\|^2=18$."}}}, {"id": "pc08", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q13", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "cov", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "Data points $x_1=(0,0)$, $x_2=(2,1)$, $x_3=(1,2)$. Standard PCA is run on this dataset. (Original Q12, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Which of the following represents the covariance matrix $C$?", "options": ["$\\dfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\dfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\dfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}0&0&0\\\\0&5&4\\\\0&4&5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\dfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}0&0&0\\\\0&2&1\\\\0&1&2\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the covariance matrix of the three raw points.</p>", "idea": "<p>Standard PCA centres the data first: $C=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\tilde x_i\\tilde x_i^T$. Equivalently $C=S-\\mu\\mu^T$ where $S=\\tfrac1n\\sum x_ix_i^T$ is the uncentred second-moment matrix.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Mean $\\mu=\\tfrac13(0+2+1,\\,0+1+2)=(1,1)\\ne0$. Centred points: $(-1,-1),(1,0),(0,1)$.</li><li>Outer products: $\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\begin{bmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$. Sum $=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$; divide by $n=3$: $C=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Option 2.</li><li>Shortcut: $\\sum x_ix_i^T=\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$, so $S=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$ (this is option 1). $\\mu\\mu^T=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}3&3\\\\3&3\\end{bmatrix}$, so $S-\\mu\\mu^T=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$.</li><li>Elimination: the data are in $\\mathbb R^2$, so $C$ is $2\\times2$, removing options 3 and 4. The trace must be $\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|\\tilde x_i\\|^2=\\tfrac{2+1+1}3=\\tfrac43$; option 1 has trace $\\tfrac{10}3$, option 2 has $\\tfrac43$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>With $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ the matrix would be $\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}$, which is in no option, so $\\tfrac1n$ is confirmed again.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $3C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$; the three methods agree; key option 2.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting to centre (option 1) or counting data points as features (options 3 and 4).", "rule": "<p>\"Standard PCA\" with raw points and non-zero mean: one option will be the uncentred $S$; use $C=S-\\mu\\mu^T$ to catch it.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(1,0),(3,2),(2,4)$. Covariance matrix ($\\tfrac1n$)? What would the uncentred version be?", "a": "$\\mu=(2,2)$; $\\tilde x=(-1,-2),(1,0),(0,2)$; $C=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}2&2\\\\2&8\\end{bmatrix}$. Uncentred $S=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}14&14\\\\14&20\\end{bmatrix}$; the difference is $\\mu\\mu^T=\\begin{bmatrix}4&4\\\\4&4\\end{bmatrix}$."}, "optWhy": ["This is the uncentred matrix $S$ (the mean was not subtracted).", "<b>Correct.</b> Centred, $\\tfrac1n$.", "Wrong size: $3\\times3$ would be for 3 features.", "Wrong size."]}}, {"id": "pc09", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "Data points $x_1=(0,0)$, $x_2=(2,1)$, $x_3=(1,2)$. Standard PCA is run on this dataset. (Original Q12, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "What is the variance of the dataset along the first principal component?", "key": {"lo": 1, "hi": 1, "show": "1"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1$ of $C=\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ from the previous question.</p>", "idea": "<p>Variance along PC1 is the largest eigenvalue of the centred covariance matrix.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\operatorname{tr}C=\\tfrac43$, $\\det C=\\tfrac19(4-1)=\\tfrac13$. So $\\lambda^2-\\tfrac43\\lambda+\\tfrac13=0$, i.e. $3\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+1=0$, $(3\\lambda-1)(\\lambda-1)=0$, giving $\\lambda=1,\\tfrac13$.</li><li>Pattern shortcut: $\\tfrac13\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\b&a\\end{bmatrix}$ with $a=2,b=1$ has eigenvalues $\\tfrac{a\\pm b}3=1,\\tfrac13$.</li><li>Projection check: $q_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$; scores of centred points are $-\\sqrt2,\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$; squares $2,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12$; mean $=1$.</li><li>So $\\lambda_1=1$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Bounds: $\\max C_{jj}=\\tfrac23\\le\\lambda_1\\le\\operatorname{tr}C=\\tfrac43$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code eigenvalues $[1,0.3333]$; key $1$.</p>", "trap": "$3$ (eigenvalue of the uncentred option), $1.5$ ($\\tfrac1{n-1}$), $\\tfrac43$ (total variance), $\\tfrac23$ (the x-axis variance).", "rule": "<p>Check the answer against $\\max C_{jj}\\le\\lambda_1\\le\\operatorname{tr}C$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "For points $(1,0),(3,2),(2,4)$, the variance along PC1 (three decimals)?", "a": "Bracket $\\begin{bmatrix}2&2\\\\2&8\\end{bmatrix}$: trace $10$, det $12$, eigenvalues $5\\pm\\sqrt{13}$; $\\lambda_1=\\tfrac{5+\\sqrt{13}}3\\approx2.869$."}}}, {"id": "pc10", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "cov", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "Dataset $D$: the eight corners $(\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1)$ of a cube of edge $2$ centred at the origin. (Original Q7, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Find the covariance matrix $C$ of the dataset. Enter the sum of all entries of $C$.", "key": {"lo": 3, "hi": 3, "show": "3"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\sum_{j,k}C_{jk}$ for the eight cube corners $(\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The mean is $0$, and the signs of the three coordinates are independent, so $C$ is diagonal. The sum of all entries also equals the variance of the sum feature $s=x_1+x_2+x_3$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Each coordinate is $+1$ four times and $-1$ four times, so $\\mu=0$.</li><li>Diagonal: $C_{jj}=\\tfrac18\\cdot8=1$.</li><li>Off-diagonal: $x_1x_2=+1$ for 4 corners and $-1$ for 4, so $C_{12}=0$; same for $C_{13},C_{23}$. So $C=I_3$ and the sum of entries is $3$.</li><li>Check via $s$: values $-3,-1,1,3$ with counts $1,3,3,1$, mean $0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(s)=\\tfrac{9+3+3+9}8=3$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$\\tfrac1{n-1}$ would give $\\tfrac87\\cdot3\\approx3.43$, which does not match the key.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $C=I_3$, sum $3$.</p>", "trap": "Dividing by nothing ($24$ or $8$), reading one entry ($1$), or confusing trace with sum of entries (equal here only by coincidence).", "rule": "<p>\"Sum of all entries of $C$\" is $\\mathbf 1^TC\\mathbf 1=\\operatorname{Var}(\\sum_jx_j)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Eight points $(\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3)$. Sum of entries of $C$? Variance along $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}(1,1,1)$?", "a": "$C=\\operatorname{diag}(1,4,9)$, sum $14$; along the unit direction $\\tfrac13\\mathbf 1^TC\\mathbf 1=\\tfrac{14}3\\approx4.667$."}}}, {"id": "pc11", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "dual", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.6 Dual PCA and mixed", "wording": "Let $A$ be a data matrix of shape $d\\times n$. The question compares the eigenvectors of $A^TA$ and $AA^T$. Statement: $A^TA$ and $AA^T$ have the same eigenvectors.", "options": ["True", "False"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> whether $A^TA$ and $AA^T$ share eigenvectors.</p>", "idea": "<p>They share non-zero eigenvalues, but their eigenvectors live in different spaces and are linked only through $A$: $u=Av/\\sqrt\\lambda$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$A^TA$ is $n\\times n$, so its eigenvectors are in $\\mathbb R^n$. $AA^T$ is $d\\times d$, so its eigenvectors are in $\\mathbb R^d$. If $d\\ne n$ they cannot even be compared.</li><li>Even if $d=n$: take $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$. $B^TB=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $BB^T=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}$ have top eigenvectors about $(0.526,0.851)$ and $(0.851,0.526)$, which differ.</li><li>So the statement is false.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>True facts to keep separate: same non-zero eigenvalues, same trace ($\\sum A_{ij}^2$), same rank.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code on $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&0\\\\0&1&1\\end{bmatrix}$: $AA^T$ eigenvalues $6,1$; $A^TA$ eigenvalues $6,1,0$; $Av/\\sqrt\\lambda$ is an eigenvector of $AA^T$. Key False.</p>", "trap": "Merging \"same eigenvalues\" (true) with \"same eigenvectors\" (false) in MSQs.", "rule": "<p>$A^TA$ vs $AA^T$: non-zero eigenvalues yes, trace yes, rank yes; eigenvectors no, size no.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$A$ is $3\\times5$ and $AA^T$ has eigenvalues $9,4,0$. All eigenvalues of $A^TA$?", "a": "Non-zero ones agree: $9,4$; $A^TA$ is $5\\times5$ with rank $2$, so three zeros: $9,4,0,0,0$."}, "optWhy": ["True would require identical eigenvectors; shapes and examples show otherwise.", "<b>Correct.</b> Same non-zero eigenvalues, different eigenvectors."]}}, {"id": "pc12", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "tagNote": "Printed key $100$ contradicts the question: the largest eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=50$. $100$ would be the total variance or an index starting at $0$. The correct value is $50$ [FLAG].", "wording": "Let $D=\\{x^{(1)},\\ldots,x^{(n)}\\}$ be a dataset of $n$ observations where each $x^{(i)}\\in\\mathbb R^{100}$. The dataset is centered: $\\sum_{i=1}^nx^{(i)}=0$. The covariance matrix has eigenvalues $\\lambda_i=100\\cdot2^{-i},\\ 1\\le i\\le100$. Let $u\\in\\mathbb R^{100}$ be the direction of maximum variance with $\\|u\\|=1$. Find $\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(u^Tx^{(i)}\\right)^2$.", "key": {"lo": 100, "hi": 100, "show": "100 (see flag; correct value 50)"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the variance along the maximum-variance direction, which is the largest eigenvalue.</p>", "idea": "<p>$\\tfrac1n\\sum(u^Tx^{(i)})^2=u^TCu$ for centred data, and its maximum over unit $u$ is $\\lambda_{\\max}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$2^{-i}$ decreases with $i$, so the largest term is at $i=1$: $\\lambda_1=100\\cdot2^{-1}=50$.</li><li>Reason: $u=\\sum c_kq_k$ gives $u^TCu=\\sum c_k^2\\lambda_k$, a weighted average, at most $\\lambda_1$; equality at $u=q_1$.</li><li>So the correct value is $50$. The key $100$ matches only two other readings: the total variance $\\sum_{i=1}^{100}\\lambda_i=100-100\\cdot2^{-100}\\approx100$, or an index starting at $i=0$ ($\\lambda_0=100$).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The question itself says the quantity equals the largest eigenvalue; with $1\\le i\\le100$ that is $50$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact fractions): $\\lambda_1=50$, $\\sum\\lambda_i\\approx100$.</p>", "trap": "$100$ (total or off-by-one), $\\approx0$ ($\\lambda_{100}$, the smallest), $\\sqrt{50}$ (standard deviation).", "rule": "<p>Eigenvalues given by a formula: read the index range, find the monotonic direction; max-variance is the largest term, total variance is the sum.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred data in $\\mathbb R^{50}$ with $\\lambda_i=90\\cdot3^{-i}$, $1\\le i\\le50$. Max variance? Total variance? PC1 share?", "a": "$\\lambda_1=30$; total $=45(1-3^{-50})\\approx45$; share $\\approx66.7\\%$."}}}, {"id": "pc13", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "dirs", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "2.2 PC directions and orthogonality", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (1, 2, 3) is derived [?].", "wording": "Which statements are true regarding applying standard PCA on a centered dataset?", "options": ["The variance of the dataset along the first principal component is maximum.", "Eigenvectors are always orthogonal to each other in PCA.", "The first principal component is the eigenvector corresponding to the largest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix.", "The first principal component is the eigenvector corresponding to the smallest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix."], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements about the PCs are true.</p>", "idea": "<p>PC1 maximises variance and is the top eigenvector; the PCs PCA picks form an orthonormal set because $C$ is symmetric.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1: PC1 $=\\arg\\max_{\\|u\\|=1}u^TCu$, so the variance along it is maximum. True.</li><li>Option 2: $C$ is symmetric, so it has an orthonormal set of eigenvectors, and PCA uses it; for distinct eigenvalues they are automatically orthogonal. True in the PCA context. (With a repeated eigenvalue, \"any two eigenvectors\" need not be orthogonal, but the PCs chosen are.)</li><li>Option 3: the maximum of $u^TCu$ is $\\lambda_{\\max}$ at its eigenvector. True.</li><li>Option 4: the smallest eigenvalue gives the last PC, not PC1. False.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Test with $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3&1\\\\1&3\\end{bmatrix}$: eigenvalues $4,2$, eigenvectors $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,\\pm1)$ with dot product $0$; the variance along the largest-eigenvalue vector is $4$, the maximum.</p>", "verify": "<p>Pairs consistent: options 1/3 true, option 4 false. If the official key were $\\{1,3\\}$ the difference would come only from the repeated-eigenvalue edge case in option 2.</p>", "trap": "Reversing largest and smallest.", "rule": "<p>PC1 $\\leftrightarrow$ largest $\\lambda$ and maximum variance; PCs are orthonormal.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred data in $\\mathbb R^3$, eigenvalues $5,5,1$. True? (a) PC1 unique up to sign (b) PC3 unique up to sign (c) variance along any unit vector in span(PC1,PC2) is $5$ (d) PC1 and PC3 are perpendicular.", "a": "(b), (c), (d). (a) is false because of the tie; in the eigenspace of $5$ the matrix acts like $5I$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b> The PCs are orthonormal.", "<b>True.</b>", "False: that is the minimum-variance direction."]}}, {"id": "pc14", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "dual", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.6 Dual PCA and mixed", "tagNote": "Option 4 is cut off in the source and cannot be judged; the key is the source marking (2, 3, 5) [?].", "context": "Data matrix $X\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times n}$ with $d\\gg n$, centred. $C=\\tfrac1nXX^T$ and the kernel matrix is $K=X^TX$. (Original Q13, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Which of the following statements are true?", "options": ["The eigenvectors of $C$ and $K$ are related by a scaling factor $r\\in\\mathbb R$.", "Computing eigenvectors using the matrix $K$ reduces the number of computations significantly than using the matrix $C$.", "Suppose the maximum eigenvalue of $C$ is $\\lambda_1$, then the maximum eigenvalue of $XX^T$ is $n\\lambda_1$.", "The eigenvectors of $C\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $K\\in\\mathbb R^d$ [wording truncated in the source]", "The eigenvalues of both $C$ and $K$ are non-negative."], "key": {"opts": [1, 2, 4]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statements about $C=\\tfrac1nXX^T$ ($d\\times d$) and $K=X^TX$ ($n\\times n$).</p>", "idea": "<p>$C$ and $K$ share non-zero eigenvalues up to the factor $n$, and eigenvectors are linked by $X$: if $K\\alpha=\\lambda\\alpha$ then $C(X\\alpha)=\\tfrac\\lambda n(X\\alpha)$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1: eigenvectors of $C$ live in $\\mathbb R^d$, of $K$ in $\\mathbb R^n$; no scalar can relate them. The link is $w=X\\alpha/\\sqrt\\lambda$, a matrix map. False.</li><li>Option 2: eigen-decomposition costs about (size)$^3$, so $n^3\\ll d^3$. True.</li><li>Option 3 (as printed, \"maximum eigenvalue of $XX^T$ is $n\\lambda_1$\"): $XX^T=nC$, so eigenvalues are multiplied by $n$. True.</li><li>Option 4: its wording is truncated in the source, so it cannot be judged (as visible it also gives $K\\in\\mathbb R^d$, which is the wrong shape).</li><li>Option 5: $u^TCu=\\tfrac1n\\|X^Tu\\|^2\\ge0$ and $\\alpha^TK\\alpha=\\|X\\alpha\\|^2\\ge0$, so both matrices are PSD. True.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Code with $d=8,n=3$: top eigenvalue of $C$ is $1.8086$, of $XX^T$ is $5.4259=3\\times1.8086$; eigenvalues of $K$ are $\\{0,3.96,5.43\\}$, all non-negative.</p>", "verify": "<p>$w=X\\alpha/\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}$ is a unit eigenvector of $C$ (residual about $7\\times10^{-16}$). Key $\\{2,3,5\\}$.</p>", "trap": "Turning \"same eigenvalues\" into \"scaled eigenvectors\", or rejecting option 3 by confusing $C$ with $XX^T$.", "rule": "<p>$C$ vs $K$: shapes $d\\times d$ and $n\\times n$; $\\lambda_K=n\\lambda_C$; eigenvectors $w\\propto X\\alpha$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$X\\in\\mathbb R^{1000\\times20}$ centred, $C=\\tfrac1{20}XX^T$, largest eigenvalue of $K$ is $60$. (a) Largest eigenvalue of $C$? (b) Minimum zero eigenvalues of $C$? (c) of $K$?", "a": "(a) $60/20=3$. (b) rank $\\le19$ after centring, so at least $981$. (c) at least $1$."}, "optWhy": ["False: different spaces.", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "Unjudgeable (truncated), not part of the key.", "<b>True.</b>"]}}, {"id": "pc15", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "dual", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.6 Dual PCA and mixed", "context": "Data matrix $X\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times n}$ with $d\\gg n$, centred. $C=\\tfrac1nXX^T$ and the kernel matrix is $K=X^TX$. (Original Q13, comprehension, 0 marks.)", "wording": "Which of the following matrices helps in transforming the eigenvectors of $K$ to the eigenvectors of $C$?", "options": ["$X$", "$X^T$", "$C^{-1}$", "$K^{-1}$", "$X^{-1}$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the matrix $M$ such that $M\\alpha$ is an eigenvector of $C$ whenever $K\\alpha=\\lambda\\alpha$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Multiply the eigen-equation of $K$ by $X$ on the left.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$X^TX\\alpha=\\lambda\\alpha$. Multiply by $X$: $XX^TX\\alpha=\\lambda X\\alpha$.</li><li>Divide by $n$: $C(X\\alpha)=\\tfrac\\lambda n(X\\alpha)$. So $X\\alpha$ is an eigenvector of $C$ (normalise by $\\sqrt\\lambda$).</li><li>Shape check: $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R^n$ must go to $\\mathbb R^d$, so the matrix is $d\\times n$: that is $X$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>\"Transform\" suggests an inverse, but here none exists since $d\\gg n$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code ($d=8,n=3$): $Cw=\\tfrac{\\lambda_K}nw$ for $w=X\\alpha/\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}$. Key $X$.</p>", "trap": "$X^T$ goes the wrong way.", "rule": "<p>$K\\to C$ is $X$ (points space to feature space); $C\\to K$ is $X^T$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$X=\\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\\\2&-2\\\\-1&1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $K$, its top eigenpair, then the unit PC1 of $C$ and the variance along it.", "a": "$K=\\begin{bmatrix}6&-6\\\\-6&6\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\lambda_K=12$, $\\alpha=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$; $w=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(1,2,-1)$; variance $12/2=6$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "$n\\times d$: maps in the opposite direction ($C\\to K$).", "$C$ has rank $\\le n<d$, so $C^{-1}$ does not exist.", "Output would be in $\\mathbb R^n$, and $K$ is singular after centring.", "$X$ is not square, so no inverse."]}}, {"id": "pc16", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "dirs", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "2.2 PC directions and orthogonality", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (option 4) is derived by orthogonality [?].", "wording": "Suppose a dataset lies in $\\mathbb R^4$ and undergoes PCA after being centered. The resulting first and second principal components are $w_1=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$, $w_2=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following could be the third principal component?", "options": ["$\\frac1{\\sqrt2}[1,1,0,0]^T$", "$\\frac1{\\sqrt3}[1,1,1,0]^T$", "$\\frac1{\\sqrt2}[-1,-1,0,0]^T$", "$\\frac1{\\sqrt3}[0,1,1,1]^T$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> a vector that can serve as PC3.</p>", "idea": "<p>PCs are orthonormal: PC3 must have unit length and be perpendicular to both $w_1$ and $w_2$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Use integer versions $a=(1,-1,0,1)$, $b=(1,0,1,-1)$.</li><li>Option 1 $(1,1,0,0)$: $\\cdot a=0$ but $\\cdot b=1$. Fails.</li><li>Option 2 $(1,1,1,0)$: $\\cdot a=0$, $\\cdot b=2$. Fails.</li><li>Option 3 $(-1,-1,0,0)$: $\\cdot b=-1$. Fails (it is also $-$option 1).</li><li>Option 4 $(0,1,1,1)$: $\\cdot a=0-1+0+1=0$, $\\cdot b=0+0+1-1=0$, length $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\sqrt3=1$. Works.</li><li>Complement check: $p-q+s=0$, $p+r-s=0$ gives $v=(s-r,2s-r,r,s)$; $r=s=1$ gives $(0,1,1,1)$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The complement is 2-dimensional, so the real PC3 depends on the data; the question asks what \"could\" be.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: dots with $w_1$ all $0$; with $w_2$: $0.408,0.667,-0.408,0$; norms all $1$.</p>", "trap": "Checking only $w_1$ (options 1 to 3 pass that test).", "rule": "<p>\"Could be PC$_k$\": perpendicular to every earlier PC, then unit length.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "In $\\mathbb R^4$ with $w_1=\\tfrac12(1,1,1,1)$, $w_2=\\tfrac12(1,-1,1,-1)$, which could be PC3? (a) $\\tfrac12(1,1,-1,-1)$ (b) $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,0,0,-1)$ (c) $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1,0,0)$ (d) $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1,0,0)$", "a": "Only (a): both dot products are $0$ and its length is $1$. (b) and (c) fail with $w_2$, (d) fails with $w_1$."}, "optWhy": ["Perpendicular to $w_1$ but not $w_2$.", "Perpendicular to $w_1$ but not $w_2$.", "Same line as option 1, same failure.", "<b>Correct.</b>"]}}, {"id": "pc17", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "dirs", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.2 PC directions and orthogonality", "tagNote": "The stem appears truncated in the source; solved under the likely reading \"zero-variance direction\" [?].", "wording": "Consider a centered dataset $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ such that every data point $(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)$ satisfies $2x_1+x_2-x_3+2x_4=0$. Which of the following vectors can represent directions associated with this dataset?", "options": ["$\\frac1{\\sqrt{10}}(2,1,-1,2)$", "$\\frac1{\\sqrt{10}}(-2,-1,1,-2)$", "$\\frac1{\\sqrt6}(1,0,2,1)$", "$\\frac13(2,0,1,2)$"], "key": {"opts": [0, 1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which vectors are valid directions for this constrained data.</p>", "idea": "<p>Every point satisfies $a^Tx=0$ with $a=(2,1,-1,2)$, so the data has zero variance along $a$. That means $Ca=0$ and $\\pm a/\\|a\\|$ is the PC with eigenvalue $0$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$a^Tx=0$ for all points gives $a^TCa=\\tfrac1n\\sum(a^Tx_i)^2=0$, so $Ca=0$.</li><li>$\\|a\\|=\\sqrt{4+1+1+4}=\\sqrt{10}$. So $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt{10}}(2,1,-1,2)$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $0$ (option 1), and so is its negative (option 2: same direction).</li><li>Options 3 and 4 are unit vectors too, but $(1,0,2,1)$ and $(2,0,1,2)$ are not multiples of $a$; they are not eigenvectors of $C$ in general (numerically residuals $0.27$ and $0.54$).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The stem looks truncated in the source (probably \"the PC with the smallest eigenvalue\"); under the plausible readings the answer is the same.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code on 200 random points in the hyperplane: $\\max|a^Tx|\\approx2\\times10^{-15}$; eigenvalues $\\{0,0.856,1.026,1.093\\}$; the zero-eigenvalue eigenvector is $a/\\sqrt{10}$.</p>", "trap": "Ticking only option 1 ($-v$ is the same direction), calling the normal the first PC (it is the smallest), or accepting unit vectors by length alone.", "rule": "<p>\"Every point satisfies $a^Tx=0$\": $\\pm a/\\|a\\|$ is the zero-variance PC.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred data in $\\mathbb R^3$ with $x_1-2x_2+2x_3=0$. Which can be a zero-eigenvalue PC? (a) $\\tfrac13(1,-2,2)$ (b) $\\tfrac13(-1,2,-2)$ (c) $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt5}(2,1,0)$ (d) $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}(1,1,1)$", "a": "(a) and (b). (c) lies inside the plane, so it can be a positive-variance PC but not a zero one; (d) is neither."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b> The zero-variance PC.", "<b>Correct.</b> Same line, opposite sign.", "Not parallel to $a$; unit length alone is not enough.", "Not parallel to $a$."]}}, {"id": "pc18", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.2 PC directions and orthogonality", "context": "Dataset $D$: the eight corners $(\\pm1,\\pm1,\\pm1)$ of a cube centred at the origin. (Original Q7.)", "wording": "Let $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be some unit vector. Find the variance of the dataset along direction $v$.", "key": {"lo": 1, "hi": 1, "show": "1"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the variance of the cube corners along any unit direction.</p>", "idea": "<p>$C=I_3$ for this dataset, so $v^TCv=\\|v\\|^2=1$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>From the cube question, $C=I_3$.</li><li>For a unit vector $v$, $v^TCv=v^Tv=1$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>With $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ you would get $\\tfrac87\\approx1.143$, which is not the key.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: for random unit $v$, $\\tfrac18\\sum(v^Tx_i)^2=1.0$. Key $1$.</p>", "trap": "$3$ (total variance), or \"depends on $v$\" (the PCs are not unique, but the variance is the same in every direction).", "rule": "<p>\"Some/any unit vector\" with a numeric answer: $C=kI$ and the answer is $k$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Box corners $(\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3)$. Variance along $v=\\tfrac13(2,1,2)$? Range over all unit $v$?", "a": "$C=\\operatorname{diag}(1,4,9)$: $\\tfrac19(4+4+36)=\\tfrac{44}9\\approx4.889$; range $[1,9]$."}}}, {"id": "pc19", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$, first two PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt5}(1,0,2)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(-2,1,1)$. The point is $[1,2,1]^T$; $(a,b)$ is its representation in the two PCs (PC-1 first). (Original Q13, 0 marks.)", "wording": "What is the value of $a$? Enter your answer correct to three decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 1.25, "hi": 1.5, "show": "1.25 to 1.5"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC-1 coordinate $a$ of the point $x=[1,2,1]$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The coordinate of a point on a unit PC $w_k$ is the dot product $z_k=w_k^T\\tilde x$ (the data are already centred). Both PCs are unit and perpendicular, so no other scaling is needed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$a=w_1^Tx=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt5}(1\\cdot1+0\\cdot2+2\\cdot1)=\\tfrac3{\\sqrt5}\\approx1.342$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Without normalising $w$ you would get a number that is too large (see the trap).</p>", "verify": "<p>Pythagoras check: with $b=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(-2+2+1)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}$ and the third coordinate $c=\\tfrac{11}{\\sqrt{30}}\\cdot(\\pm1)$: $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\tfrac95+\\tfrac16+\\tfrac{121}{30}=6=\\|x\\|^2$. Code agrees.</p>", "trap": "Using the un-normalised vector (this gives a wrong value outside the accepted range), or dividing by $\\|u\\|^2$ (that gives the coefficient on the un-normalised vector, not the coordinate).", "rule": "<p>Coordinate on PC $k$ $=w_k^Tx$ with $\\|w_k\\|=1$; $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\|x\\|^2$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "PCs $w_1=\\tfrac13(2,1,2)$, $w_2=\\tfrac13(1,2,-2)$, centred point $x=(3,0,3)$. Find $(a,b)$ and the information lost when only 2 PCs are kept.", "a": "$a=\\tfrac{6+6}3=4$, $b=\\tfrac{3-6}3=-1$. $w_3=\\tfrac13(-2,2,1)$, $c=-1$, lost $c^2=1$. Check $16+1+1=18=9+0+9$."}}}, {"id": "pc20", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$, first two PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt5}(1,0,2)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(-2,1,1)$. The point is $[1,2,1]^T$; $(a,b)$ is its representation in the two PCs (PC-1 first). (Original Q13, 0 marks.)", "wording": "What is the value of $b$? Enter your answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.25, "hi": 0.55, "show": "0.25 to 0.55"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC-2 coordinate $b$ of the point $x=[1,2,1]$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The coordinate of a point on a unit PC $w_k$ is the dot product $z_k=w_k^T\\tilde x$ (the data are already centred). Both PCs are unit and perpendicular, so no other scaling is needed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$b=w_2^Tx=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(-2\\cdot1+1\\cdot2+1\\cdot1)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}\\approx0.408$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Without normalising $w$ you would get a number that is too large (see the trap).</p>", "verify": "<p>Reconstruction $aw_1+bw_2=(0.267,0.167,1.367)$; the residual has squared norm $4.033=c^2$, as expected.</p>", "trap": "Using the un-normalised vector (this gives a wrong value outside the accepted range), or dividing by $\\|u\\|^2$ (that gives the coefficient on the un-normalised vector, not the coordinate).", "rule": "<p>Coordinate on PC $k$ $=w_k^Tx$ with $\\|w_k\\|=1$; $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\|x\\|^2$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Same PCs as the transfer above and $x=(3,0,3)$: what is $b$ and its sign?", "a": "$b=-1$; keep the sign that the dot product gives."}}}, {"id": "pc21", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$, first two PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}(1,1,1)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1,0)$. The point is $[2,1,-1]^T$; $(a,b)$ is its representation in the two PCs (PC-1 first). (Original Q6, 0 marks.)", "wording": "What is the value of $a$? Enter your answer correct to three decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 1.1, "hi": 1.2, "show": "1.10 to 1.20"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC-1 coordinate $a$ of the point $x=[2,1,-1]$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The coordinate of a point on a unit PC $w_k$ is the dot product $z_k=w_k^T\\tilde x$ (the data are already centred). Both PCs are unit and perpendicular, so no other scaling is needed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$a=w_1^Tx=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}(2+1-1)=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}\\approx1.155$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Without normalising $w$ you would get a number that is too large (see the trap).</p>", "verify": "<p>Pythagoras: $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\tfrac43+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{25}6=6=\\|x\\|^2$. Reconstruction $aw_1+bw_2=(\\tfrac76,\\tfrac16,\\tfrac23)$, residual $\\tfrac56(1,1,-2)$, exactly along the third PC.</p>", "trap": "Using the un-normalised vector (this gives a wrong value outside the accepted range), or dividing by $\\|u\\|^2$ (that gives the coefficient on the un-normalised vector, not the coordinate).", "rule": "<p>Coordinate on PC $k$ $=w_k^Tx$ with $\\|w_k\\|=1$; $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\|x\\|^2$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1,0)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(1,-1,2)$, point $(4,2,2)$. Find $(a,b)$ and compare $a^2+b^2$ with $\\|x\\|^2$.", "a": "$a=3\\sqrt2\\approx4.243$, $b=\\sqrt6\\approx2.449$; $a^2+b^2=24=\\|x\\|^2$, so nothing is lost: the point lies in the plane of the two PCs."}}}, {"id": "pc22", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$, first two PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}(1,1,1)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1,0)$. The point is $[2,1,-1]^T$; $(a,b)$ is its representation in the two PCs (PC-1 first). (Original Q6, 0 marks.)", "wording": "What is the value of $b$? Enter your answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.65, "hi": 0.75, "show": "0.65 to 0.75"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC-2 coordinate $b$ of the point $x=[2,1,-1]$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The coordinate of a point on a unit PC $w_k$ is the dot product $z_k=w_k^T\\tilde x$ (the data are already centred). Both PCs are unit and perpendicular, so no other scaling is needed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$b=w_2^Tx=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(2\\cdot1-1\\cdot1+0)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\approx0.707$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Without normalising $w$ you would get a number that is too large (see the trap).</p>", "verify": "<p>Same Pythagoras identity as above; the third coordinate squared is $\\tfrac{25}6$.</p>", "trap": "Using the un-normalised vector (this gives a wrong value outside the accepted range), or dividing by $\\|u\\|^2$ (that gives the coefficient on the un-normalised vector, not the coordinate).", "rule": "<p>Coordinate on PC $k$ $=w_k^Tx$ with $\\|w_k\\|=1$; $a^2+b^2+c^2=\\|x\\|^2$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "With the transfer PCs above, what is the third coordinate for $(4,2,2)$?", "a": "$0$: the point has no component outside the two PCs."}}}, {"id": "pc23", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ of a mean-centered dataset; the first two PCs are $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$ and $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-1,1)$. (Original Q9.)", "wording": "Find the coordinates of the point $\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}$ in the new coordinate system formed by the principal components.", "options": ["$\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt2\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC coordinates of $(2,2)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Coordinates are $(w_1^Tx,\\,w_2^Tx)$ because the PCs are orthonormal.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$a=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(2+2)=2\\sqrt2$.</li><li>$b=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-2+2)=0$. So $(2\\sqrt2,0)$.</li><li>Check: $2\\sqrt2\\cdot\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)=(2,2)$; also $a^2+b^2=8=\\|x\\|^2$. The point lies exactly on PC1.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$(2,2)$ is a multiple of $w_1$, so all other coordinates vanish.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $W^{-1}x=(2.828,0)$. Key option 1.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the $\\sqrt2$ in the unit PC and answering $(2,0)$.", "rule": "<p>A point along PC1 has zero coordinates on every other PC.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Same PCs, point $(4,0)$: coordinates?", "a": "$a=\\tfrac4{\\sqrt2}=2.828$, $b=\\tfrac{-4}{\\sqrt2}=-2.828$; not on an axis. $8+8=16$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "Would be right only if $x$ were $(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2})$-scaled; the PC length is not $\\|x\\|$.", "Wrong: the point is on PC1, so the PC2 coordinate is $0$, not $1$.", "$2$ is the raw coordinate, not the projection ($\\sqrt2$ times it)."]}}, {"id": "pc24", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.6 Dual PCA and mixed", "tagNote": "The stem does not state that the data are centred; solved with the standard assumption [?].", "wording": "Let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ be $d$-dimensional data points $(d>n)$, and let $X$ be the $d\\times n$ matrix containing the data points. The $k$-th largest eigenvalue and corresponding unit eigenvector of $X^TX$ are $\\lambda$ and $\\alpha_k$, respectively. What will be the projection of $x_i$ onto the $k$-th principal component?", "options": ["$x_i^T\\alpha_k$", "$\\dfrac{x_i^T\\alpha_k}{\\sqrt{n\\lambda}}$", "$\\dfrac{x_i^TX\\alpha_k}{\\sqrt{\\lambda}}$", "$x_i^TX\\alpha_k$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the PC coordinate of $x_i$ in terms of the Gram-matrix eigenpair $(\\lambda,\\alpha_k)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Dual PCA: the unit PC in $\\mathbb R^d$ is $w_k=X\\alpha_k/\\sqrt\\lambda$. The projection (coordinate) of $x_i$ is $x_i^Tw_k$. (Assumes the data is centred, the PCA standard.)</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$X^TX\\alpha=\\lambda\\alpha$. Then $w=X\\alpha$ satisfies $\\|w\\|^2=\\alpha^TX^TX\\alpha=\\lambda$, so the unit vector is $w_k=X\\alpha_k/\\sqrt\\lambda$.</li><li>Projection $=x_i^Tw_k=\\dfrac{x_i^TX\\alpha_k}{\\sqrt\\lambda}$. Option 3.</li><li>Check with the training-point shortcut: $x_i^TX\\alpha=(X^TX\\alpha)_i=\\lambda\\alpha_i$, so the score is $\\sqrt\\lambda\\,\\alpha_{k,i}$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The stem does not say the data are centred; the standard PCA assumption is used, and the key agrees.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code ($d=6,n=4$, centred): option 3 equals $x_i^Tw$ to about $10^{-16}$; option 4 is bigger by $\\sqrt\\lambda=2.01$.</p>", "trap": "Dropping the normalisation or using $n$ inside the square root.", "rule": "<p>Dual PCA: $w=X\\alpha/\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}$, score of training point $i$ is $\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}\\,\\alpha_i$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred $X$ with $n=4$, top eigenvalue of $X^TX$ is $8$, unit eigenvector $\\alpha=\\tfrac12(1,1,-1,-1)$. (a) PC1 coordinate of $x_2$? (b) variance of the PC1 scores?", "a": "(a) $\\sqrt8\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\sqrt2\\approx1.414$. (b) $\\lambda_K/n=\\tfrac84=2$."}, "optWhy": ["$\\alpha_k\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$: the dot product is not even defined when $d\\ne n$.", "$\\sqrt{n\\lambda}$ is wrong: $\\lambda$ here is for $X^TX$, so the norm is $\\sqrt\\lambda$.", "<b>Correct.</b>", "Missing the $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt\\lambda}$ normalisation; the result is too large by $\\sqrt\\lambda$."]}}, {"id": "pc25", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "wording": "Let $X$ be a data matrix of shape $(d,n)$ for a centered dataset. The first principal component of the dataset is $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\\\\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$. What will be the scalar projection of the point $\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\4\\end{bmatrix}$ onto the first principal component? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 4.92, "hi": 4.98, "show": "4.92 to 4.98"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the scalar projection of $(3,4)$ onto PC1.</p>", "idea": "<p>For a unit vector $w_1$ the scalar projection is $w_1^Tx$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\|w_1\\|^2=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12=1$, so it is already unit.</li><li>$z_1=\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2(3+4)=\\tfrac{7\\sqrt2}2=4.9497\\approx4.95$.</li><li>Check: projection vector $z_1w_1=(3.5,3.5)$ with length $3.5\\sqrt2=4.9497$; also $24.5+0.5=25=\\|x\\|^2$ with the perpendicular part $\\tfrac{|{-3}+4|}{\\sqrt2}=0.707$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Reading $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ as $\\tfrac12$ would give $3.5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $4.9497$, inside the accepted range.</p>", "trap": "Using $\\|x\\|=5$ (the point is not on PC1), or multiplying by $\\sqrt2$ again ($9.90$).", "rule": "<p>Scalar projection on a unit vector is just the dot product (it can be negative).</p>", "transfer": {"q": "PC1 $=(0.6,0.8)$, point $(5,-5)$. Scalar projection, its length, the projection vector?", "a": "$3-4=-1$; length $1$; vector $(-0.6,-0.8)$."}}}, {"id": "pc26", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$ with PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1,0)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-1,1,0)$, $w_3=(0,0,1)$. The first two are retained and the third discarded. The point is $(1,2,3)$ and its representation in the first two PCs is $(a,b)$. (Original Q10.)", "wording": "Find $a$. Enter the answer correct to three decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 2.071, "hi": 2.171, "show": "2.071 to 2.171"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the first PC coordinate of $(1,2,3)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>$a=w_1^Tx$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$a=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1+2+0)=\\tfrac3{\\sqrt2}=2.121$.</li><li>The third entry of $w_1$ is $0$, so the $z$-coordinate $3$ does not contribute.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$c=w_3^Tx=3$ is the discarded coordinate.</p>", "verify": "<p>Pythagoras: $4.5+0.5+9=14=1+4+9$. Reconstruction $aw_1+bw_2=(1,2,0)$, residual $(0,0,3)$.</p>", "trap": "Adding $3$ as well: $\\tfrac{1+2+3}{\\sqrt2}=4.243$.", "rule": "<p>Use each PC\\'s own entries; zeros mean that coordinate does not contribute.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(0,1,1)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(0,-1,1)$, $w_3=(1,0,0)$, point $(4,1,3)$, keep two PCs. $(a,b)$ and the squared information lost?", "a": "$a=2\\sqrt2=2.828$, $b=\\sqrt2=1.414$, $c=4$; lost $c^2=16$."}}}, {"id": "pc27", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "coord", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.3 Projection scores", "context": "Centred dataset in $\\mathbb R^3$ with PCs $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1,0)$, $w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-1,1,0)$, $w_3=(0,0,1)$. The first two are retained and the third discarded. The point is $(1,2,3)$ and its representation in the first two PCs is $(a,b)$. (Original Q10.)", "wording": "Find $b$. Enter the answer correct to three decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.657, "hi": 0.757, "show": "0.657 to 0.757"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the second PC coordinate of $(1,2,3)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>$b=w_2^Tx$, keeping the sign of $w_2$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$b=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-1+2+0)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}=0.707$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Flipping the sign of $w_2$ would give $-0.707$, outside the range.</p>", "verify": "<p>Reconstruction with two PCs is $(1,2,0)$; the whole loss is the $z$-coordinate ($c^2=9$).</p>", "trap": "Wrong sign, or including the $z$-coordinate.", "rule": "<p>Follow the sign of the PC exactly as printed.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Same transfer PCs ($w_2=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(0,-1,1)$) and point $(4,1,3)$: what is $b$?", "a": "$\\tfrac{-1+3}{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2\\approx1.414$."}}}, {"id": "pc28", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "evr", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.4 Explained variance and rank", "wording": "Consider a dataset of 50 points, all of which lie in $\\mathbb R^5$. The eigenvalues of the covariance matrix are: $1.7,\\ 1.4,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.20,\\ 0.005$. If PCA is run and the top-$k$ principal components are retained, what is a good choice of $k$? Hint: A good PCA should capture at least 95% of the variance.", "key": {"lo": 4, "hi": 4, "show": "4"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the smallest $k$ whose cumulative explained variance is at least $95\\%$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Total variance is the sum of eigenvalues. Cumulative explained variance (CEV) after $k$ PCs is the sum of the top $k$ eigenvalues divided by the total.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Total $=1.7+1.4+0.25+0.20+0.005=3.555$.</li><li>CEV: $k=1$: $1.7/3.555=47.8\\%$; $k=2$: $3.1/3.555=87.2\\%$; $k=3$: $3.35/3.555=94.23\\%$; $k=4$: $3.55/3.555=99.86\\%$.</li><li>$94.23\\%<95\\%$, so $k=3$ is not enough (\"at least 95%\" is not \"about 95%\"). $k=4$ is the first to pass: $k=4$.</li><li>Error view: the discarded variance equals the average squared reconstruction error. At $k=3$ it is $0.205$ ($5.77\\%>5\\%$); at $k=4$ it is $0.005$ ($0.14\\%$).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Both views agree: $1-0.9423=0.0577=0.205/3.555$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code cumulative ratios $[0.478,0.872,0.9423,0.9986,1]$. Key $4$.</p>", "trap": "Rounding $94.23\\%$ up to $95\\%$ and answering $3$, or using the elbow and answering $2$.", "rule": "<p>Add eigenvalues from the largest until the running share reaches the threshold; \"at least\" means it must reach it.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Eigenvalues $8,4,2,1,0.5,0.5$. Smallest $k$ for $90\\%$? for $95\\%$? Average squared error at the $95\\%$ choice?", "a": "Total $16$; cumulative $50,75,87.5,93.75,96.875,100\\%$. $90\\%$: $k=4$; $95\\%$: $k=5$; error $=\\lambda_6=0.5$ ($3.125\\%$ of $16$)."}}}, {"id": "pc29", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q16", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "evr", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "2.4 Explained variance and rank", "tagNote": "Read literally the stem implies nothing strict; the key (option 2) holds on the reading \"about $d-1$ PCs are needed\" [?].", "context": "Dataset $X\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times n}$, $d\\gg n$, centred; $C=\\tfrac1nXX^T$, $K=X^TX$. (Original Q13.)", "wording": "Suppose that 95% of the variance of the data is captured using $d-1$ eigenvectors. This implies that:", "options": ["The features are highly correlated (or dependent).", "The features have low correlation (or independent)."], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> what \"95% of the variance needs $d-1$ eigenvectors\" implies about the features.</p>", "idea": "<p>Needing almost all $d$ PCs means the variance is spread out nearly evenly, with no dominant direction. Strongly correlated features instead give one dominant PC and need very few components.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Strongly correlated features: for two features with $\\rho=0.98$ the eigenvalues are $1.98$ and $0.02$; PC1 alone explains $99\\%$. So correlation lowers the number of PCs needed.</li><li>A flat spectrum needs many PCs: with $d=21$ features of equal variance, $k=19$ gives $90.5\\%$ and $k=20=d-1$ gives $95.2\\%$. Equal variance and no correlation is the case that matches the statement.</li><li>So the statement points to low correlation (option 2), not high correlation (option 1).</li><li>Caution: read literally the stem implies nothing strict. Option 2 is the intended reading (\"about $d-1$ PCs are needed\"), and it matches the key.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Independence alone is not enough: independent features with variances $100,1,1$ need only $k=1$ for $95\\%$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: the flat example gives $k^*=20$; the $\\rho=0.98$ pair gives PC1 $99\\%$.</p>", "trap": "Reading \"only $d-1$\" as \"few components suffice\" and choosing option 1.", "rule": "<p>Many PCs needed $\\Rightarrow$ flat spectrum $\\Rightarrow$ low correlation; few PCs needed $\\Rightarrow$ correlated features.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "(a) $d=21$, all variances $1$, uncorrelated. (b) $d=3$, variances $100,1,1$, uncorrelated. Smallest $k$ for $95\\%$ in each?", "a": "(a) $k=20$ ($\\tfrac{19}{21}=0.905$, $\\tfrac{20}{21}=0.952$). (b) $k=1$ ($\\tfrac{100}{102}=0.980$). Independence alone does not force a large $k$."}, "optWhy": ["High correlation would let a few PCs capture most variance, the opposite of the stem.", "<b>Correct</b> on the intended reading: variance is spread out."]}}, {"id": "pc30", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "evr", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.4 Explained variance and rank", "wording": "The first five eigenvalues of $X^TX$ are: $10,\\ 5,\\ 3,\\ 2,\\ 1$ and all remaining eigenvalues are zero. What is the proportion of variance explained along the first principal component? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.45, "hi": 0.5, "show": "0.45 to 0.50"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1/\\sum_j\\lambda_j$ for the Gram-matrix eigenvalues.</p>", "idea": "<p>The non-zero eigenvalues of $X^TX$ equal $n$ times those of $C$, so the ratio $\\lambda_1/\\sum\\lambda$ is the same for $C$ and $X^TX$: the factor $n$ cancels.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Sum $=10+5+3+2+1=21$ (the zeros add nothing).</li><li>Proportion $=\\tfrac{10}{21}=0.4762\\approx0.48$.</li><li>Check with $C$ (say $n=100$): eigenvalues $0.1,0.05,\\ldots$, total $0.21$, ratio $0.1/0.21=0.4762$. Shares: $47.6,23.8,14.3,9.5,4.8\\%$ sum to $100$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Dividing only the numerator by $n$ gives a wrong $0.0048$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $0.47619$. Key range $0.45$ to $0.50$.</p>", "trap": "Giving a percentage ($47.62$) when the range is a decimal, or dividing only one side by $n$.", "rule": "<p>Explained variance ratio uses eigenvalues of $C$ or $X^TX$ alike; $n$ cancels.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Non-zero eigenvalues of $X^TX$ are $9,4,2,1$, $n=50$. (a) share of PC2? (b) cumulative share of top two? (c) largest eigenvalue of $C$?", "a": "Total $16$. (a) $0.25$. (b) $0.8125$. (c) $9/50=0.18$."}}}, {"id": "pc31", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "recon", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (2, 3, 4) is derived; option 2 is exact only if the points span the subspace [?].", "wording": "Consider a dataset of 2000 points all of which lie in $\\mathbb R^{30}$. If all these points lie in four dimensional low subspace then after applying the PCA algorithm which of the following statements is/are true?", "options": ["The covariance matrix corresponding to this dataset has 30 non-zero eigenvalues.", "The covariance matrix corresponding to this dataset has only four non-zero eigenvalues.", "The residues will become zero after four rounds.", "By utilizing four principal component vectors and their corresponding coefficients, we have the ability to reconstruct the complete dataset using representatives."], "key": {"opts": [1, 2, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements hold when the data lie in a 4-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb R^{30}$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The number of non-zero eigenvalues equals the dimension of the subspace spanned (the rank). Each PCA round removes one direction, so the residue reaches zero after that many rounds, and that many PCs reconstruct everything.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (30 non-zero eigenvalues): 30 is the number of features, not the rank. False.</li><li>Option 2 (only four non-zero eigenvalues): the data span a 4-dimensional subspace, so rank $=4$. True (strictly \"at most four\"; exactly four when the 2000 points span the subspace, as generic data do).</li><li>Option 3 (residues zero after four rounds): after projecting out four orthogonal directions nothing is left. True.</li><li>Option 4 (four PCs and their coefficients reconstruct the complete dataset): $\\hat x=\\sum_{j\\le4}z_jw_j$ is exact when the residue is zero. True.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Numeric model: a random 4-dim subspace of $\\mathbb R^{30}$ with 2000 centred points has eigenvalues $24.84,8.81,3.84,0.98$ and the rest about $0$; the maximum residue after rounds 1 to 4 is $5.68,3.76,1.51,8\\times10^{-15}$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code confirms 4 non-zero eigenvalues and zero residue at round 4.</p>", "trap": "Equating the number of features with the number of eigenvalues that matter.", "rule": "<p>Rank = subspace dimension = non-zero eigenvalues = rounds until zero residue.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "3000 points in $\\mathbb R^{40}$ lie in a 7-dim subspace (and span it). (a) non-zero eigenvalues? (b) rounds until zero residue? (c) numbers stored at $k=7$ and the percentage decrease?", "a": "(a) $7$ (b) $7$ (c) $7(40+3000)=21280$ vs $120000$, decrease $82.27\\%$."}, "optWhy": ["False: $d=30$ is not the rank.", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b> Lossless compression."]}}, {"id": "pc32", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "recon", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "wording": "Let $w_1$ and $w_2$ be the top two principal components of the covariance matrix of a centered dataset. Let $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ be a data point. Which expression gives the reconstruction error of $x$ after projecting it onto the top two principal components?", "options": ["$\\left\\|x-(x^Tw_1)w_1-(x^Tw_2)w_2\\right\\|^2$", "$\\left\\|x-(x^Tw_1)w_1\\right\\|^2+\\left\\|x-(x^Tw_2)w_2\\right\\|^2$", "$(x^Tw_1)^2+(x^Tw_2)^2$", "$\\|x-w_1\\|^2+\\|x-w_2\\|^2$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the reconstruction error of $x$ when two PCs are kept.</p>", "idea": "<p>Reconstruct first with both PCs together, $\\hat x=(x^Tw_1)w_1+(x^Tw_2)w_2$, then the error is $\\|x-\\hat x\\|^2$: the squared length of what is left over.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\hat x=(x^Tw_1)w_1+(x^Tw_2)w_2$, so the error is $\\|x-(x^Tw_1)w_1-(x^Tw_2)w_2\\|^2$ (option 1).</li><li>Option 2 subtracts one PC at a time and adds two separate errors; each term still contains the other PC\\'s part, so the loss is double counted.</li><li>Option 3 is the captured part (Pythagoras: $\\|x\\|^2=\\text{error}+(x^Tw_1)^2+(x^Tw_2)^2$), not the error.</li><li>Option 4 subtracts the unit vectors $w_j$ themselves, not the projections.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Test with $\\tilde x=(3,1)$ and PCs $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1),\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(-1,1)$ (full $\\mathbb R^2$): option 1 $=0$, option 2 $=10$, option 3 $=10$, option 4 $=19.17$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Identity check with $x=(3,4,12)$, $w_j=e_j$: option 1 $=144$, option 3 $=25$, $144+25=169=\\|x\\|^2$.</p>", "trap": "Taking the captured part (option 3) as the error.", "rule": "<p>Error $=\\|x-\\hat x\\|^2$ with all kept PCs subtracted at once.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$w_1=e_1$, $w_2=e_2$ in $\\mathbb R^3$, $x=(3,4,12)$. Values of options 1, 2, 3 and $\\|x\\|^2$?", "a": "Option 1 $=144$, option 2 $=313$, option 3 $=25$, $\\|x\\|^2=169$; options 1 and 3 add up to $\\|x\\|^2$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "Double counts: each term removes only one PC.", "This is the captured variance part, not the error.", "$w_j$ is a direction, not the projection of $x$."]}}, {"id": "pc33", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "recon", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "tagNote": "Option 3 as extracted has a comma instead of an operator; judged as written [?].", "wording": "Which of the following expressions is the reconstruction error for a dataset of $n$ points with respect to a line passing through the origin represented by unit vector $w$, where $\\|w\\|=1$?", "options": ["$\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left\\|x_i-(x_i^Tw)w\\right\\|^2$", "$\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n[x_i-(x_i^Tw)w]^T[x_i-(x_i^Tw)w]$", "$\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n[x_i^Tx_i,\\ (x_i^Tw)^2]$", "$-\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i^Tw)^2$"], "key": {"opts": [0, 1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which expressions equal the average squared distance of the points from the line along $w$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Projection of $x_i$ onto the line is $(x_i^Tw)w$; the error is the average squared length of $x_i-(x_i^Tw)w$. Since $\\|a\\|^2=a^Ta$, the norm form and the product form are the same number.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 is exactly $\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|x_i-(x_i^Tw)w\\|^2$: correct.</li><li>Option 2 writes the same squared length as $a^Ta$ for $a=x_i-(x_i^Tw)w$: correct (it is a scalar).</li><li>Option 3 as printed has a comma between $x_i^Tx_i$ and $(x_i^Tw)^2$, so it is a 2-entry list, not a scalar: cannot be the error. (If the comma were a minus it would equal the error by Pythagoras, but the source marks only options 1 and 2.)</li><li>Option 4 is $-\\tfrac1n\\sum(x_i^Tw)^2$, i.e. minus the captured variance; the error is $V_{\\text{tot}}-w^TCw$, so a constant $V_{\\text{tot}}$ is missing.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Identity: $E(w)=V_{\\text{tot}}-w^TCw$. For points $(\\pm2,0),(0,\\pm1)$: $w=(1,0)$ gives $0.5=2.5-2$; $w=(0,1)$ gives $2.0=2.5-0.5$; option 4 gives $-2$ and $-0.5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Random 5-D check: $\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|x-(x^Tw)w\\|^2=3.4254=\\tfrac1n\\sum\\|x\\|^2-\\tfrac1n\\sum(x^Tw)^2$.</p>", "trap": "Treating $a^Ta$ as a matrix, or taking negative captured variance as the error.", "rule": "<p>Error along $w$ is $V_{\\text{tot}}-w^TCw$; minimising error means maximising variance.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(\\pm2,0),(0,\\pm1)$. (a) $E$ for $w=(1,0)$ (b) for $w=(0,1)$ (c) which is smaller and how does it link to $V_{\\text{tot}}$ and $\\lambda$?", "a": "(a) $0.5$ (b) $2.0$ (c) $w=(1,0)$ with $E=V_{\\text{tot}}-\\lambda_1=2.5-2=0.5=\\lambda_2$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "<b>Correct.</b>", "A list of two numbers, not an error (as printed).", "Missing the constant $V_{\\text{tot}}$; it is negative."]}}, {"id": "pc34", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "storage", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "wording": "Consider a dataset with 1000 samples, where each sample belongs to $\\mathbb R^{20}$. PCA is run and the top 5 principal components are retained, while the rest are discarded. If it takes one unit of memory to store a real number, find the percentage decrease in storage space of the dataset by moving to its compressed representation. Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 74, "hi": 75, "show": "74 to 75"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the percentage saved by storing $k=5$ PCs and scores instead of the raw data.</p>", "idea": "<p>Raw storage is $dn$ numbers. Compressed storage is $k$ directions of $d$ numbers each plus $k$ scores for each of $n$ points: $k(d+n)$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Raw: $20\\times1000=20000$.</li><li>Compressed: $5\\times(20+1000)=5100$.</li><li>Decrease $=\\tfrac{20000-5100}{20000}=0.745=74.50\\%$.</li><li>If the mean vector ($20$ numbers) is also stored, compressed $=5120$ and the decrease is $74.40\\%$; both lie in the accepted range.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Beware accidental passes: storing only the scores ($5000$) gives $75.00\\%$, and $1-\\tfrac kd=75\\%$; these are conceptually wrong.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $74.50\\%$ (or $74.40\\%$ with the mean).</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the PC directions ($kd$) or the scores ($kn$).", "rule": "<p>Storage $=k(d+n)$ versus $dn$; saving only while $k(d+n)<dn$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$n=300$ samples in $\\mathbb R^{60}$, $k=6$. (a) Percentage decrease? (b) Largest integer $k$ with strictly positive saving?", "a": "(a) raw $18000$, compressed $6\\cdot360=2160$, decrease $88.00\\%$. (b) break-even $k=50$, so $k\\le49$."}}}, {"id": "pc35", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "storage", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "wording": "Consider a dataset $\\{x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_{50}\\},\\ x_i\\in\\mathbb R^{1000}$. After performing PCA on the dataset, suppose that the residues become zero after the fifth round. Then, how many numbers do we need to store for this dataset?", "key": {"lo": 5250, "hi": 5250, "show": "5250"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the storage after lossless compression.</p>", "idea": "<p>Residues vanish after $k=5$ rounds, so rank $=5$: five PCs (each with $d$ numbers) and five scores per point suffice.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Compressed storage $=k(d+n)=5\\times(1000+50)=5250$.</li><li>Sanity: raw is $50000$; rank bound $5\\le\\min(1000,49)$ holds.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The key does not count a stored mean (that would give $6250$).</p>", "verify": "<p>$5\\times1050=5250$, key $5250$.</p>", "trap": "$5\\times1000$ or $5\\times50$ alone, or giving the raw $50000$.", "rule": "<p>Zero residue after $k$ rounds $\\Rightarrow$ store $k(d+n)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$n=200$ points in $\\mathbb R^{500}$, residues zero after $8$ rounds. Numbers stored and the decrease?", "a": "$8\\times700=5600$ vs $100000$: decrease $94.4\\%$."}}}, {"id": "pc36", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "recon", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.5 Reconstruction, residuals, storage", "context": "Data $x_1=(0,0)$, $x_2=(2,1)$, $x_3=(1,2)$; standard PCA. Mean $(1,1)$, centred points $(-1,-1),(1,0),(0,1)$, $\\lambda_1=1$ with $w_1=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$, $\\lambda_2=\\tfrac13$. (Original Q12, comprehension.)", "wording": "Find the residues after projecting the data points onto the first principal component. Here $x_i'$ represents the residue of the data point $x_i$.", "options": ["$x_1'=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},x_2'=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5\\\\0.5\\end{bmatrix},x_3'=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5\\\\0.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$x_1'=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},x_2'=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5\\\\-0.5\\end{bmatrix},x_3'=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5\\\\0.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$x_1'=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},x_2'=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix},x_3'=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$", "$x_1'=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},x_2'=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix},x_3'=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the residue of each point after removing its PC1 component.</p>", "idea": "<p>Residue $=$ centred point $-$ its projection on $w_1$; it is perpendicular to $w_1$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Centred points: $(-1,-1),(1,0),(0,1)$. Scores $w_1^T\\tilde x$: $-\\sqrt2,\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$.</li><li>Projections $z\\,w_1$: $(-1,-1),(0.5,0.5),(0.5,0.5)$.</li><li>Residues: $(0,0)$, $(1,0)-(0.5,0.5)=(0.5,-0.5)$, $(0,1)-(0.5,0.5)=(-0.5,0.5)$. Option 2.</li><li>Check: all residues are perpendicular to $w_1$ (dot products $0$), and the average $\\|r\\|^2=\\tfrac{0+0.5+0.5}3=\\tfrac13=\\lambda_2$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Only option 2 has zero dot product with $w_1$ for all points.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: dot products with $w_1$: option 1 $(0,0.707,0.707)$, option 2 $(0,0,0)$, option 3 $(0,2.12,2.12)$, option 4 $(0,-0.707,-0.707)$.</p>", "trap": "Writing the projection as the residue.", "rule": "<p>Residue $\\perp$ kept PCs and $\\|r\\|^2\\le\\|\\tilde x\\|^2$; average residue $=$ sum of dropped eigenvalues.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(3,0),(0,3)$, PC1 $=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$ ($\\lambda_1=3,\\lambda_2=1$). Residues and their average $\\|r\\|^2$?", "a": "Centred $(-1,-1),(2,-1),(-1,2)$; residues $(-1,-1),(0.5,0.5),(0.5,0.5)$; average $\\tfrac{2+0.5+0.5}3=1=\\lambda_2$."}, "optWhy": ["These are the projections, not the residues.", "<b>Correct.</b>", "Too long: $\\|x_2'\\|^2=5>\\|\\tilde x_2\\|^2=1$, impossible.", "Not perpendicular to $w_1$ (dot product $-0.707$)."]}}, {"id": "pc37", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "rank", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "2.4 Explained variance and rank", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (2, 4) is derived, assuming the points span the subspace [?].", "wording": "Consider a dataset consisting of 5000 points in $\\mathbb R^{50}$. All points lie in a 6-dimensional linear subspace. Which of the following will be true after applying PCA?", "options": ["There will be 50 non-zero eigenvalues of the covariance matrix.", "There will be 6 non-zero eigenvalues of the covariance matrix.", "Using the first 5 principal components, the dataset can be reconstructed with zero residue.", "The variance captured by the top 6 principal components equals the total variance of the dataset."], "key": {"opts": [1, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements hold for data in a 6-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb R^{50}$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Rank $=6$ (assuming the points span the subspace): six non-zero eigenvalues, the top six PCs carry all variance, and six PCs (not five) are needed for zero residue.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (50 non-zero eigenvalues): false, rank is $6$.</li><li>Option 2 (6 non-zero eigenvalues): true (exactly 6 if the points span the subspace; at most 6 in general).</li><li>Option 3 (five PCs give zero residue): false; dropping one needed direction leaves residue $\\lambda_6>0$.</li><li>Option 4 (top 6 capture total variance): true, since the remaining eigenvalues are $0$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Numeric model: 6 non-zero eigenvalues, $\\lambda_6=0.995$ (so $k=5$ leaves error $0.995$), $\\lambda_7\\approx10^{-14}$, top six share $1.0000$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code confirms options 2 and 4.</p>", "trap": "Counting features instead of rank, or confusing 5 with 6.", "rule": "<p>Rank $r$: $r$ non-zero eigenvalues, $\\text{CEV}(r)=1$, zero residue exactly at $k\\ge r$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "2000 points in $\\mathbb R^{25}$ in a 3-dim subspace. True? (i) 3 non-zero eigenvalues (ii) top 2 PCs give zero residue (iii) top 3 PCs hold all variance (iv) CEV(2)=1.", "a": "(i) and (iii) true; (ii) and (iv) false (generic spanning data)."}, "optWhy": ["False: that is the number of features.", "<b>True.</b>", "False: five PCs leave $\\lambda_6>0$ error.", "<b>True.</b>"]}}, {"id": "pc38", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "rank", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.4 Explained variance and rank", "wording": "Consider a mean-centered dataset consisting of 150 patients from the healthcare domain with 6 features. The dataset is represented as a $150\\times6$ matrix $X$. PCA is performed and it is observed that the reconstruction error becomes zero after completing $k$ components. A domain expert provides: the set $C_1,C_2,C_3$ is linearly independent; the following relationships hold: $C_4=2C_1-C_2$, $C_5=C_2+C_3$, $C_6=4C_1-3C_5$. What is the value of $k$?", "key": {"lo": 3, "hi": 3, "show": "3"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the number of components needed for zero reconstruction error, which is the rank of the centred data matrix.</p>", "idea": "<p>Zero error needs as many PCs as the rank, i.e. the number of linearly independent feature columns. Here $C_j$ are the six feature columns.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$C_1,C_2,C_3$ are independent (given).</li><li>$C_4=2C_1-C_2$ and $C_5=C_2+C_3$ lie in their span; $C_6=4C_1-3C_5=4C_1-3C_2-3C_3$ is also in the span.</li><li>So the column space has dimension $3$: $k=3$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Equivalent: the $3\\times6$ relation matrix has rank $3$, giving nullity $3$ and rank $6-3=3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Numeric: random $C_1,C_2,C_3\\in\\mathbb R^{150}$ with the relations gives eigenvalues $36.25,3.41,1.13,0,0,0$.</p>", "trap": "Answering $6$ (features) or $150$ (patients); also do not count dependent columns as the answer (here it coincides at $3$, but with $4$ independent and $2$ dependent columns $k$ would be $4$).", "rule": "<p>Zero reconstruction error after $k$ components means $k=\\operatorname{rank}$ = independent columns.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "80 samples, 5 features, centred. $D_1,D_2$ independent; $D_3=D_1+D_2$, $D_4=3D_1-2D_3$, $D_5=D_4-D_2$. Components for zero error?", "a": "Everything is in span$\\{D_1,D_2\\}$ ($D_4=D_1-2D_2$, $D_5=D_1-3D_2$), so $k=2$."}}}, {"id": "pc39", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "dual", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "2.6 Dual PCA and mixed", "tagNote": "The printed eigenvector $(2,3,4,6)/\\sqrt{54}$ is not unit; the intended last entry is $5$. The answer uses only entry 2, so it is unaffected [?].", "wording": "Consider a dataset $X$ of shape $(d,n)$, with $d>n$. The $k$-th principal component can be written as $w_k=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_ix_i$, where $x_i$ is the $i$-th data point. The $k$-th largest eigenvalue and corresponding eigenvector of $X^TX$ are $10$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac2{\\sqrt{54}}\\\\\\frac3{\\sqrt{54}}\\\\\\frac4{\\sqrt{54}}\\\\\\frac6{\\sqrt{54}}\\end{bmatrix}$, respectively. What will be the value of $\\alpha_2$? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.12, "hi": 0.13, "show": "0.12 to 0.13"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the coefficient $\\alpha_2$ in $w_k=\\sum\\alpha_ix_i$.</p>", "idea": "<p>In dual PCA, if $\\beta$ is a unit eigenvector of $K=X^TX$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda$, then the unit PC is $w=X\\beta/\\sqrt\\lambda=\\sum_i\\tfrac{\\beta_i}{\\sqrt\\lambda}x_i$, so $\\alpha_i=\\beta_i/\\sqrt\\lambda$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Why: $\\|X\\beta\\|^2=\\beta^TX^TX\\beta=\\lambda$, so dividing by $\\sqrt\\lambda$ makes $w$ unit.</li><li>$\\alpha_2=\\dfrac{\\beta_2}{\\sqrt{10}}$ with $\\beta_2=\\tfrac3{\\sqrt{54}}$: $\\alpha_2=\\tfrac{3}{\\sqrt{540}}=\\tfrac{3}{23.238}=0.1291\\approx0.13$, inside the accepted range.</li><li>Note: the printed vector $(2,3,4,6)/\\sqrt{54}$ has squared norm $\\tfrac{4+9+16+36}{54}=\\tfrac{65}{54}\\ne1$; the intended vector is $(2,3,4,5)/\\sqrt{54}$ (squared norm $1$). The answer depends only on the second entry, so it is unaffected.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Index check: $\\alpha_2$ uses the second entry $3/\\sqrt{54}$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: a 4-point dataset in $\\mathbb R^6$ built from the intended vector and $\\lambda=10$ gives $w$ of length $1$, an eigenvector of $XX^T$, and $\\alpha_2=0.1291$.</p>", "trap": "$\\alpha_2=\\beta_2$ ($0.408$), $\\beta_2/\\lambda$ ($0.0408$), $\\beta_2\\sqrt\\lambda$ ($1.29$).", "rule": "<p>Dual PCA: $\\alpha=\\beta/\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\lambda_K=24$, $\\beta=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt6}(1,1,-2)$. Find $\\alpha_1,\\alpha_3$ and the PC1 variance if $n=3$.", "a": "$\\sqrt{24}=4.899$; $\\alpha_1=\\tfrac1{12}=0.0833$, $\\alpha_3=-\\tfrac2{12}=-0.1667$; variance $24/3=8$."}}}, {"id": "pc40", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q10", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "cov", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "tagNote": "Figure 1 is not in the source; the key is matched through the sibling answers (4.5 and 0.5) [?].", "context": "Dataset 1 (Figure 1, not reproduced): four centred 2-D points. A consistent reconstruction is $(1,2),(2,1),(-1,-2),(-2,-1)$, which reproduces the keys of the sibling questions (hypothesis, not the source figure).", "wording": "What is the covariance matrix of the dataset?", "options": ["$\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}2.5&2\\\\2&2.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}3.5&2.5\\\\2.5&3.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}4&3\\\\3&4\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the covariance matrix of Dataset 1.</p>", "idea": "<p>The sibling answers fix the eigenvalues $4.5$ and $0.5$ (with $\\operatorname{tr}C=5$). Use $C=\\tfrac1n\\sum x_ix_i^T$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>With the reconstructed points: $\\sum x_1^2=1+4+1+4=10$, $\\sum x_1x_2=2+2+2+2=8$, so $C=\\tfrac14\\begin{bmatrix}10&8\\\\8&10\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2.5&2\\\\2&2.5\\end{bmatrix}$ (option 2).</li><li>Eigenvalues of $\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\b&a\\end{bmatrix}$ are $a\\pm b=4.5,0.5$, matching the next two questions.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Unnormalised $XX^T$ or $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ gives $\\begin{bmatrix}10&8\\\\8&10\\end{bmatrix}$ or $\\begin{bmatrix}3.33&2.67\\\\2.67&3.33\\end{bmatrix}$, neither in the options.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: eigenvalues $[4.5,0.5]$; the Gram matrix has eigenvalues $18,2$, and $18/4=4.5$.</p>", "trap": "Picking the large clean-looking option 1 (wrong divisor).", "rule": "<p>$C=\\tfrac1nXX^T$; eigenvalues of $K$ divided by $n$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Centred points $(3,1),(1,3),(-3,-1),(-1,-3)$. $C$? PC1 variance from $K$'s eigenvalue $32$?", "a": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}5&3\\\\3&5\\end{bmatrix}$; $32/4=8=5+3$."}, "optWhy": ["Twice option 2: the divisor is $n/2$ instead of $n$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Eigenvalues $4.5,0.5$.", "Eigenvalues $6,1$: no match.", "Eigenvalues $7,1$: no match."]}}, {"id": "pc41", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "Dataset 1 (Figure 1, not reproduced): four centred 2-D points. A consistent reconstruction is $(1,2),(2,1),(-1,-2),(-2,-1)$, which reproduces the keys of the sibling questions (hypothesis, not the source figure).", "wording": "If standard PCA is performed on this dataset, what is the variance along the first principal component? Enter the answer correct to one decimal place.", "key": {"lo": 4.5, "hi": 4.5, "show": "4.5"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_1$ of $C=\\begin{bmatrix}2.5&2\\\\2&2.5\\end{bmatrix}$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Variance along PC$_k$ is $\\lambda_k$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Pattern $a\\pm b$ with $a=2.5,b=2$: $\\lambda=4.5,0.5$.</li><li>Check: $u=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$: $u^TCu=\\tfrac12(2.5+2+2+2.5)=4.5$; trace $5=4.5+0.5$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The Gram eigenvalue is $18$, so $18/n=18/4=4.5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $Cu=4.5u$.</p>", "trap": "$2.5$ (the x-axis variance) or $18$ (forgetting to divide by $n$).", "rule": "<p>\"Along PC1\": the largest eigenvalue.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}4&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. PC2 variance (three decimals)?", "a": "$\\lambda=3\\pm\\sqrt2$; the smaller is $1.586$."}}}, {"id": "pc42", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "var", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "2.1 Covariance, eigenvalues, variance along PCs", "context": "Dataset 1 (Figure 1, not reproduced): four centred 2-D points. A consistent reconstruction is $(1,2),(2,1),(-1,-2),(-2,-1)$, which reproduces the keys of the sibling questions (hypothesis, not the source figure).", "wording": "If standard PCA is performed on this dataset, what is the variance along the second principal component? Enter the answer correct to one decimal place.", "key": {"lo": 0.5, "hi": 0.5, "show": "0.5"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_2$ of the same $C$.</p>", "idea": "<p>$\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2=\\operatorname{tr}C$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\lambda_2=a-b=2.5-2=0.5$, or $5-4.5=0.5$.</li><li>Check: $v=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$ gives $Cv=0.5v$; $\\det=4.5\\cdot0.5=2.25=2.5^2-2^2$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>From the Gram matrix: $2/4=0.5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $Cv=0.5v$.</p>", "trap": "$2$ (the off-diagonal entry) or $2$ from the Gram eigenvalue without dividing by $n$.", "rule": "<p>\"Second PC variance\": the smaller eigenvalue, $\\operatorname{tr}C-\\lambda_1$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "For $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5&3\\\\3&5\\end{bmatrix}$ find the PC2 variance and check with $K$'s smaller eigenvalue $8$ ($n=4$).", "a": "$5-3=2$; $8/4=2$."}}}], "test": [{"id": "t1", "concept": "cov", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(1,2),(3,4),(5,6)$. What is $C_{11}$ (variance of feature 1, divide by $n$)? 3 decimals.", "num": {"ans": 2.667, "tol": 0.002, "show": "2.667"}, "explain": "Centred first coordinates $-2,0,2$: $(4+0+4)/3=8/3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t2", "concept": "evr", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Eigenvalues $6,3,1$. Explained variance ratio of the first component?", "num": {"ans": 0.6, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.6"}, "explain": "$6/10=0.6$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t3", "concept": "evr", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Eigenvalues $5,3,1,1$. Cumulative ratio of the first two components?", "num": {"ans": 0.8, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.8"}, "explain": "$8/10=0.8$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t4", "concept": "dirs", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "For a covariance matrix $C$ with eigenvalues $9,4,1$ and any unit vector $u$, which is always true?", "options": [{"t": "$u^TCu=9$", "ok": false, "why": "Only for the top eigenvector."}, {"t": "$1\\le u^TCu\\le9$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: between smallest and largest eigenvalue."}, {"t": "$u^TCu\\ge4$", "ok": false, "why": "Not for directions near the smallest eigenvector."}, {"t": "$u^TCu=14$", "ok": false, "why": "$14$ is the trace, not a variance along one direction."}]}, {"id": "t5", "concept": "coord", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\mu=(3,4)$, $u=(1,1)/\\sqrt2$, $x=(4,6)$. Score $z$? 3 decimals.", "num": {"ans": 2.121, "tol": 0.002, "show": "2.121"}, "explain": "$z=((4-3)+(6-4))/\\sqrt2=3/\\sqrt2=2.121$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t6", "concept": "recon", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Eigenvalues $5,3,1,1$; keep 2 components. Average squared reconstruction error?", "num": {"ans": 2, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2"}, "explain": "Sum of dropped eigenvalues: $1+1=2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t7", "concept": "rank", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=4$ points in $d=10$ dimensions. Maximum number of non-zero eigenvalues of $C$?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$\\min(d,n-1)=\\min(10,3)=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t8", "concept": "dirs", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which statement about PC directions is true?", "options": [{"t": "PC1 and PC2 need not be orthogonal", "ok": false, "why": "Symmetric matrices have orthogonal eigenvectors."}, {"t": "PC1 is unique including its sign", "ok": false, "why": "$-u$ is equally valid."}, {"t": "PC directions are orthogonal and PC1 is unique up to sign (when $\\lambda_1>\\lambda_2$)", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "Eigenvalues of $C$ can be negative", "ok": false, "why": "$C$ is PSD."}]}, {"id": "t9", "concept": "storage", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=100$, $d=10$. Numbers stored for $k=2$ components (scores plus directions, no mean)?", "num": {"ans": 220, "tol": 0.001, "show": "220"}, "explain": "$2\\cdot100+2\\cdot10=220$ versus $1000$ originally.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t10", "concept": "dual", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Gram matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$ ($n=2$). Variance of PC1 (dual PCA)?", "num": {"ans": 4.5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4.5"}, "explain": "Largest eigenvalue $9$; variance $9/n=4.5$.", "hint": ""}], "checklist": [{"id": "c1", "t": "I centre first, use 1/n, and know variance along PC_k, an axis, and the total."}, {"id": "c2", "t": "I can get coordinates, residues and reconstruction error from unit PCs."}, {"id": "c3", "t": "I know rank, zero eigenvalues and the k(d+n) storage count."}, {"id": "c4", "t": "I can use dual PCA: w = Xα/sqrt(λ), λ_C = λ_K/n."}], "formulas": [{"name": "Covariance", "tex": "C=\\frac1n\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)(x_i-\\mu)^T", "note": "Centre first; 1/n in this course."}, {"name": "Variance along unit u", "tex": "u^TCu", "note": "PC$_k$ gives $\\lambda_k$; axis $j$ gives $C_{jj}$."}, {"name": "Explained variance", "tex": "\\frac{\\lambda_1+\\dots+\\lambda_k}{\\sum_j\\lambda_j}", "note": "Cumulative share."}, {"name": "Storage", "tex": "k(d+n)", "note": "Versus $dn$ raw."}, {"name": "Dual PCA", "tex": "w=\\frac{X\\alpha}{\\sqrt{\\lambda_K}},\\ \\lambda_C=\\frac{\\lambda_K}n", "note": "K=X^TX."}]};